WN.lb(11,{"mt_9m2jSzGnvC":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Slope −1/3 is perpendicular to slope…","choices":["3","−3","1/3"],"answer":0,"hint":"Flip −1/3 upside down and change its sign.","explain":"Flipping −1/3 gives −3, and changing the sign gives 3. Check: −1/3 × 3 = −1."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"Line through (0, {c}) parallel to y = {m}x + 1. What is its y-intercept?","answer":"c","hint":"In y = mx + c, the y-intercept c is where the line crosses the y-axis.","explain":"The line passes through (0, {c}), so its y-intercept is {c}.","params":{"m":[2,6],"c":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"y = 2x + 5 and y = −0.5x are…","choices":["perpendicular","parallel","neither"],"answer":0,"hint":"Multiply the two slopes, 2 and −0.5, and see if you get −1.","explain":"2 × (−0.5) = −1, so the lines are perpendicular."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"A line with slope {m} passes through (1, {b}). A parallel line passes through (0, 0). What is its y-value at x = 2?","answer":"2*m","hint":"The parallel line has slope {m} and goes through (0, 0), so it is y = {m}x.","explain":"The line is y = {m}x, so at x = 2, y = {m} × 2 = {=2*m}.","params":{"m":[2,6],"b":[1,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"y = 4x and y = 4x are…","choices":["the same line","parallel and different","perpendicular"],"answer":0,"hint":"Compare both the slopes and the intercepts of the two lines.","explain":"Both lines have the same slope and the same intercept, so they are the same line."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"y = x + 1 and y = −x + 1 are…","choices":["perpendicular","parallel","neither"],"answer":0,"hint":"Multiply the two slopes, 1 and −1, and see if you get −1.","explain":"1 × (−1) = −1, so the lines are perpendicular."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"y = 2x and y = 3x are…","choices":["neither","parallel","perpendicular"],"answer":0,"hint":"Slopes differ, product 6.","explain":"Different slopes, product not −1."}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"A line has slope {m}. What slope does a parallel line have?","answer":"m","hint":"Parallel lines never meet, so they rise at the same rate.","explain":"Parallel lines have the same slope, so the answer is {m}.","params":{"m":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"A line has slope 2. Which slope is perpendicular?","choices":["−1/2","1/2","−2"],"answer":0,"hint":"Flip the slope upside down and change its sign.","explain":"Flip 2 to get 1/2 and change the sign to get −1/2. Check: 2 × (−1/2) = −1."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"Line A has slope {m}. Line B is perpendicular. What is slope A × slope B?","answer":-1,"hint":"Always the same.","explain":"Perpendicular slopes multiply to −1.","params":{"m":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"y = 3x + 1 and y = 3x − 4 are…","choices":["parallel","perpendicular","the same line"],"answer":0,"hint":"Compare the numbers in front of x in both lines.","explain":"Both lines have slope 3 but different intercepts, so they are parallel."}],"explain":{"kid":["Two lines that never meet are parallel. They have the same steepness, the same slope.","Lines that meet at a right angle are perpendicular. If one has slope 2, the other has slope −1/2."],"adult":["If y = m₁x + c₁ and y = m₂x + c₂, the lines are parallel when m₁ = m₂, and perpendicular when m₁ × m₂ = −1, so m₂ = −1/m₁: flip the fraction and change the sign.","To write a line through (a, b) parallel to y = 3x + 1, keep m = 3: y − b = 3(x − a). For a perpendicular line, use m = −1/3 instead. Vertical and horizontal lines are the special case with no defined product."]},"familyTip":"Draw y = 2x on squared paper, then a line through (0, 3) that crosses it at a right angle. Check its slope is −1/2.","keyIdea":"Parallel lines have equal slopes; perpendicular lines have slopes that multiply to −1.","visual":{"type":"slopes","caption":"Blue line has slope 2. Change the green slope: find the parallel one and the perpendicular one.","opts":{"m":2,"start":1}},"lab":{"type":"graphlab","caption":"Make a line parallel to y = 2x + 1 that passes through the targets: keep a = 2, change b.","opts":{"f":"a*x+b","a":1,"b":0,"xr":[-5,5],"yr":[-5,9],"targets":[[0,-2],[2,2]]}}},"mt_hVyRYu6omo":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"Work out x² − 4x when x = −1.","answer":5,"hint":"Substitute in brackets: (−1)² − 4 × (−1). Then power, multiply, subtract.","explain":"Put x = −1 in brackets: (−1)² − 4 × (−1) = 1 − (−4) = 1 + 4 = 5."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"Work out −x² when x = {a}.","answer":"-(a*a)","hint":"Square the value first, then put the minus sign in front of the answer.","explain":"({a})² = {=a*a}, then the minus in front gives {=-(a*a)}.","params":{"a":[-8,-2]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"Work out {b}x + 7 when x = {a}.","answer":"b*a+7","hint":"{b}x means {b} × x. Multiply by the value in brackets, then add 7.","explain":"{b} × ({a}) + 7 = {=b*a} + 7 = {=b*a+7}.","params":{"a":[-5,-1],"b":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"A calculator shows −9 when you type − 3 x². Why?","choices":["It squares 3 first, then applies the minus: −3² = −(3²)","The calculator is wrong, because −3 squared is 9","Squaring a negative always gives a negative"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about which comes first: the power or the minus sign in front.","explain":"The power comes before a minus in front, so −3² = −(3 × 3) = −9. To square negative three, type the brackets: (−3)² = 9."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which keys make a calculator square negative three and show 9?","choices":["( − 3 ) x²","− 3 x²","3 x² −"],"answer":0,"hint":"The minus sign has to be inside the part that gets squared.","explain":"Brackets keep the minus with the 3, so (−3)² = (−3) × (−3) = 9. Without brackets the calculator works out −(3²) = −9."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"Work out 3 − x² when x = −5.","answer":-22,"hint":"Square −5 first. The square is positive, and then it is taken away from 3.","explain":"3 − (−5)² = 3 − 25 = −22. The square is 25, and taking 25 from 3 gives −22."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"Work out 4x² − 1 when x = −1/2.","answer":0,"hint":"Square −1/2 first: multiply the tops and the bottoms. Then multiply by 4 and take away 1.","explain":"Square first: (−1/2)² = 1/4, so 4 × 1/4 − 1 = 1 − 1 = 0."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Sam works out 2x² for x = −3 as (2 × −3)² = 36. What is right?","choices":["2x² = 2 × (−3)² = 18, because the power is only on x","36 is right","2x² = −18, because x is negative"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at what the little 2 is attached to. Is it x, or 2x?","explain":"The power touches x only: (−3)² = 9, then 2 × 9 = 18. Sam squared 2x, which is a different expression, (2x)²."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m9","prompt":"Work out (x + 1)² when x = {a}.","answer":"(a+1)*(a+1)","hint":"Brackets first: work out x + 1, then square that answer.","explain":"{a} + 1 = {=a+1}, and then ({=a+1})² = {=(a+1)*(a+1)}.","params":{"a":[-6,-2]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"Work out x² when x = {a}.","answer":"a*a","hint":"Put the value in brackets and multiply it by itself. Count the minus signs.","explain":"({a})² = ({a}) × ({a}) = {=a*a}. A negative times a negative is positive.","params":{"a":[-9,-1]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"What is −x² when x = −4?","choices":["−16","16","8"],"answer":0,"hint":"The power comes before the minus in front. Find x² first, then make it negative.","explain":"x² = (−4)² = 16, and the minus in front makes it −16. Only (−x)² would give 16."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"Work out 5 − 2x when x = {a}.","answer":"5-2*a","hint":"Write 5 − 2 × ({a}). Multiply first, then take the result away from 5.","explain":"5 − 2 × ({a}) = 5 − ({=2*a}) = {=5-2*a}. Taking away a negative adds.","params":{"a":[-6,-1]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Work out 2x² − 5x when x = −2.","answer":18,"hint":"The power is only on x. Work out (−2)² first, then each multiplication, then subtract.","explain":"Put x = −2 in brackets: 2 × (−2)² − 5 × (−2) = 2 × 4 − (−10) = 8 + 10 = 18."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p5","prompt":"Work out x³ when x = {a}.","answer":"a*a*a","hint":"A cube multiplies three copies of the number. Count how many minus signs there are.","explain":"({a})³ = ({a}) × ({a}) × ({a}) = {=a*a*a}. Three negatives multiplied give a negative.","params":{"a":[-4,-1]}}],"explain":{"kid":["To evaluate an expression, swap each letter for its number. If the number is negative, write it in brackets. So 5 − 2x with x = −3 becomes 5 − 2 × (−3).","Then work it out in order. Do powers first. Next, multiply. Last, add and subtract, from left to right. Taking away a negative is like adding: 5 − (−6) = 5 + 6 = 11.","A negative times a negative is positive. So (−3)² = (−3) × (−3) = 9. A square can never be negative.","Watch a minus sign in front. −x² means: find x², then make it negative. With x = −3, that gives −9, not 9."],"adult":["Evaluating an expression means substituting a value for each variable and then simplifying with the usual order of operations: brackets, powers, multiplication and division, then addition and subtraction from left to right. Writing a substituted value in brackets, as in 2 × (−3), keeps its sign attached and shows each operation clearly.","A power binds tighter than a minus sign in front. So −x² means −(x²): with x = −3, x² = 9 and −x² = −9, while (−x)² = 9. Written numbers follow the same rule, so −3² = −9 but (−3)² = 9.","Even powers of a negative number are positive and odd powers are negative, because each pair of negative factors makes a positive: (−2)³ = −8 and (−2)⁴ = 16. Subtracting a negative adds its size, so 1 − (−4) = 5. Fractions work the same way: for x = −1/2, 4x² − 1 = 4 × 1/4 − 1 = 0.","Common mistakes: squaring a negative and keeping the minus sign, reading −x² as (−x)², losing the sign while substituting, turning a − (−b) into a − b, and treating 2x² as (2x)². On a calculator, type the brackets: without them, the calculator squares 3 first and shows −9 for −3²."]},"familyTip":"Pick a value together, such as x = −2, and each write down x², −x² and x³ before comparing answers. Then check on a calculator, once with brackets around −2 and once without, and talk about why the results differ.","keyIdea":"Put each value in brackets, then follow the order of operations. A power acts only on what it touches: for x = −3, x² = 9 but −x² = −9.","worked":{"title":"Evaluate x² − 3x + 2 when x = −4","steps":["Put −4 in brackets wherever x is: (−4)² − 3 × (−4) + 2.","Power first: (−4)² = (−4) × (−4) = 16.","Then multiply: 3 × (−4) = −12, so the line is 16 − (−12) + 2.","Taking away −12 adds 12: 16 + 12 + 2 = 30."]},"visual":{"type":"plot","caption":"Drag x along the graph of x² − 4x into the negatives. Each output is positive there: x² is positive, and −4x is too.","opts":{"mode":"eval","f":"x*x-4*x","a":-1,"xr":[-3,5],"yr":[-5,22],"step":0.5}},"lab":{"type":"flagsub","caption":"Flagship lab: drag a value into an expression, then work it out one step at a time. Tap a step too early and the lab says what must come first.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_d3V050rm1F":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"Two numbers multiply to {=a*b} and add to {=a+b}. The smaller one is {a}. What is the larger one?","answer":"b","hint":"Find the number that goes with {a} to make {=a*b}. Then check the sum is {=a+b}.","explain":"{a} × {b} = {=a*b} and {a} + {b} = {=a+b}, so the larger number is {b}.","params":{"a":[1,4],"b":[5,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"Expand (x − {a})(x − {b}). What is the constant term?","answer":"a*b","hint":"The constant term is the last number times the last number. Two negatives multiply to a positive.","explain":"(−{a}) × (−{b}) = {=a*b}, so the constant term is +{=a*b}.","params":{"a":[1,6],"b":[2,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Factorise x² − 9.","choices":["(x + 3)(x − 3)","(x − 3)(x − 3)","It cannot be factorised"],"answer":0,"hint":"Here b = 0. You need two numbers that multiply to −9 and add to 0.","explain":"3 × (−3) = −9 and 3 + (−3) = 0, so x² − 9 = (x + 3)(x − 3). The x terms cancel."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Ali says x² + 5x + 6 = (x + 5)(x + 6). What went wrong?","choices":["He copied b and c into the brackets","Nothing, it is right","He should have used (x − 5)(x − 6)"],"answer":0,"hint":"Expand (x + 5)(x + 6) and compare it with x² + 5x + 6.","explain":"(x + 5)(x + 6) = x² + 11x + 30. The numbers must multiply to 6 and add to 5, so the answer is (x + 2)(x + 3)."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Factorise x² + 2x − 8.","choices":["(x + 4)(x − 2)","(x − 4)(x + 2)","(x + 8)(x − 1)"],"answer":0,"hint":"Find a pair that multiplies to −8 and adds to +2. The larger number takes the sign of b.","explain":"4 × (−2) = −8 and 4 + (−2) = 2. The pair −4 and 2 makes −8 too, but it adds to −2."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which pair should you look for to factorise x² − x − 12?","choices":["Multiply to −12, add to −1","Multiply to −1, add to −12","Multiply to 12, add to 1"],"answer":0,"hint":"In x² + bx + c the pair multiplies to c and adds to b. Read b and c with their signs.","explain":"Here b = −1 and c = −12. The pair is −4 and 3, so x² − x − 12 = (x − 4)(x + 3)."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"x² + 8x + 15 = (x + 3)(x + k). What is k?","answer":5,"hint":"3 times k must make 15, and 3 plus k must make 8.","explain":"3 × 5 = 15 and 3 + 5 = 8, so k = 5. Expanding (x + 3)(x + 5) gives x² + 8x + 15."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Sam factorises x² − x − 6 as (x + 3)(x − 2). How can Sam check it?","choices":["Expand it: x² + x − 6, so it is wrong","Put x = 0 in both: they agree, so it is right","It looks right, so no check is needed"],"answer":0,"hint":"Multiply the brackets out and compare every term, not just one value.","explain":"(x + 3)(x − 2) = x² + x − 6, so the middle sign is wrong. The answer is (x − 3)(x + 2). x = 0 only checks the constant."}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"x² + {=a+b}x + {=a*b} = (x + {a})(x + ?). What is the missing number?","answer":"b","hint":"The two numbers must multiply to {=a*b} and add to {=a+b}. Divide {=a*b} by {a}.","explain":"{a} × {b} = {=a*b} and {a} + {b} = {=a+b}, so the missing number is {b}.","params":{"a":[1,5],"b":[2,7]},"where":"a<b"},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Factorise x² + 7x + 12.","choices":["(x + 3)(x + 4)","(x + 2)(x + 6)","(x + 7)(x + 12)"],"answer":0,"hint":"List the pairs that multiply to 12, then find the one that adds to 7.","explain":"3 × 4 = 12 and 3 + 4 = 7. The pair 2 and 6 also makes 12, but it adds to 8."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"x² + {=a-b}x − {=a*b} = (x + {a})(x − ?). What is the missing number?","answer":"b","hint":"The constant is negative, so the signs are different. You need {a} × (−?) = −{=a*b}.","explain":"{a} × (−{b}) = −{=a*b} and {a} + (−{b}) = {=a-b}, so the missing number is {b}.","params":{"a":[3,8],"b":[1,6]},"where":"a-b>1"},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Factorise x² − 2x − 15.","choices":["(x − 5)(x + 3)","(x + 5)(x − 3)","(x − 5)(x − 3)"],"answer":0,"hint":"The two numbers multiply to −15 and add to −2. Which one has to be negative?","explain":"−5 × 3 = −15 and −5 + 3 = −2. The pair 5 and −3 has the right product but adds to +2."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Factorise x² − 7x + 10.","choices":["(x − 2)(x − 5)","(x + 2)(x + 5)","(x + 2)(x − 5)"],"answer":0,"hint":"The constant is positive but the middle is negative. What does that say about both signs?","explain":"Both numbers are negative: −2 × −5 = 10 and −2 + −5 = −7. (x + 2)(x + 5) gives +7x."}],"explain":{"kid":["Factorising puts brackets back. It is expanding run in reverse. (x + 3)(x + 4) expands to x² + 7x + 12, so x² + 7x + 12 factorises to (x + 3)(x + 4).","Look at the last number first. The two numbers must multiply to it. For 12, the pairs are 1 and 12, 2 and 6, and 3 and 4.","Now look at the middle number. Pick the pair that adds to it. Only 3 and 4 add to 7, so the answer is (x + 3)(x + 4).","Signs matter. If the last number is negative, one number is negative and one is positive. If it is positive but the middle is negative, both are negative. So x² − 2x − 15 = (x − 5)(x + 3)."],"adult":["Expanding (x + p)(x + q) gives x² + (p + q)x + pq. So factorising x² + bx + c means finding integers p and q with p + q = b and pq = c. The order of the brackets does not matter: (x + 3)(x + 4) and (x + 4)(x + 3) are the same.","A reliable method is to list the factor pairs of c, including negative pairs, and test each sum. The signs narrow the search. If c is positive, p and q share the sign of b. If c is negative, they have opposite signs, and the larger size takes the sign of b.","Algebra tiles show why the method works. The x² tile and the c unit tiles fit into one rectangle only when the x strips split into p along one side and q along the other. Negative strips cancel positive ones in zero pairs, which is how x² + x − 6 can hold 3 positive and 2 negative strips.","Common mistakes: writing (x + b)(x + c), as in (x + 5)(x + 6) for x² + 5x + 6; choosing a pair with the right product but the wrong sum; flipping both signs; and thinking x² − 9 cannot be factorised because b = 0. It is (x + 3)(x − 3). Some expressions, such as x² + x + 1, do not factorise over the integers at all. Always expand your answer to check it."]},"familyTip":"Take turns choosing two whole numbers in secret, say 4 and −3, and tell the other person only their product and their sum (−12 and 1). They write the quadratic x² + x − 12 and factorise it. Then swap, and check by expanding.","keyIdea":"To factorise x² + bx + c, find two numbers that multiply to c and add to b. They go in the brackets: x² + 7x + 12 = (x + 3)(x + 4), because 3 × 4 = 12 and 3 + 4 = 7. Expand the brackets to check.","worked":{"title":"Factorise x² − 2x − 15","steps":["Pairs that multiply to −15: 1 and −15, −1 and 15, 3 and −5, −3 and 5.","c is negative, so the signs are different. b is −2, so the larger number is negative.","3 + (−5) = −2. That is the pair.","x² − 2x − 15 = (x + 3)(x − 5). Check: x² − 5x + 3x − 15 = x² − 2x − 15."]},"visual":{"type":"plot","caption":"Drag x along the graph of x² + x − 6 = (x − 2)(x + 3). It is zero at x = 2 and at x = −3, where one bracket is zero.","opts":{"mode":"eval","f":"x*x+x-6","a":0,"xr":[-5,4],"yr":[-8,16],"step":0.5}},"lab":{"type":"flagfact","caption":"Flagship lab: drag the corner of an algebra-tile rectangle until it matches the quadratic. The corner block must make c, and the strips must add to b.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_y2BWYSKFFm":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"Expand −{a}(x + {b}). What is the number term?","answer":"-(a*b)","hint":"Multiply −{a} by +{b}. A negative times a positive is negative.","explain":"−{a} × {b} = {=-(a*b)}, so −{a}(x + {b}) = −{a}x − {=a*b}.","params":{"a":[2,7],"b":[1,6]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"Expand −{a}(2 − x). What number goes in front of x?","answer":"a","hint":"Multiply −{a} by −x. Two negatives make a positive.","explain":"−{a} × (−x) = +{a}x, so the number in front of x is {a}.","params":{"a":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Jo writes −3(x − 4) = −3x − 4. What went wrong?","choices":["Jo did not multiply the −4 by −3","Jo should have added 3","Nothing, it is right"],"answer":0,"hint":"Check that the number outside multiplied every term inside the bracket.","explain":"Both terms are multiplied: −3 × (−4) = +12, so −3(x − 4) = −3x + 12."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Simplify 5 − 2(x + 1).","choices":["3 − 2x","3x + 3","7 − 2x"],"answer":0,"hint":"Multiply first. The multiplier is −2, so the +1 becomes −2.","explain":"5 − 2x − 2 = 3 − 2x. 3x + 3 subtracts before multiplying, and 7 − 2x gets the sign of −2 × 1 wrong."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Expand −(2x − 7).","choices":["−2x + 7","−2x − 7","2x + 7"],"answer":0,"hint":"Multiply both terms by −1.","explain":"−1 × 2x = −2x and −1 × (−7) = +7, so −(2x − 7) = −2x + 7."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"Simplify 7 − (x − 1). What is the number term?","answer":8,"hint":"The minus in front means −1 × (x − 1). What is −1 × (−1)?","explain":"7 − x + 1 = 8 − x, so the number term is 8."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Expand −2(3x + 4).","choices":["−6x − 8","−6x + 8","−5x − 8"],"answer":0,"hint":"Multiply −2 by 3x, then −2 by 4. Multiply, do not add.","explain":"−2 × 3x = −6x and −2 × 4 = −8. −5x adds −2 and 3x instead of multiplying."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"How can you check that 10 − 2(x − 3) = 16 − 2x?","choices":["Put in a value: x = 1 gives 14 both ways","Check that both sides have an x","Check that both sides have a 2"],"answer":0,"hint":"Equal expressions give the same value for every x you try.","explain":"10 − 2(1 − 3) = 10 + 4 = 14 and 16 − 2 = 14. One value can catch a mistake; two values make a check stronger."}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"Expand −{a}(x − {b}). What is the number term?","answer":"a*b","hint":"Multiply −{a} by −{b}. Two negatives make a positive.","explain":"−{a} × (−{b}) = +{=a*b}, so −{a}(x − {b}) = −{a}x + {=a*b}.","params":{"a":[2,6],"b":[1,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Expand −(x + 5).","choices":["−x − 5","−x + 5","x − 5"],"answer":0,"hint":"A minus in front means −1 times every term in the bracket.","explain":"−1 × x = −x and −1 × 5 = −5, so −(x + 5) = −x − 5. Both signs flip, not just the first."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"Expand and simplify {a} − 2(x + {b}). What is the number term?","answer":"a-2*b","hint":"Multiply by −2 first: −2 × {b}. Then add that to {a}.","explain":"{a} − 2x − {=2*b} = {=a-2*b} − 2x, so the number term is {=a-2*b}.","params":{"a":[5,12],"b":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Expand −4(3 − 2x).","choices":["−12 + 8x","−12 − 8x","12 − 8x"],"answer":0,"hint":"Multiply −4 by 3, then −4 by −2x. Count the minus signs in each.","explain":"−4 × 3 = −12 and −4 × (−2x) = +8x, so the answer is −12 + 8x."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Simplify 10 − 2(x − 3).","choices":["16 − 2x","4 − 2x","8x − 24"],"answer":0,"hint":"The multiplier is −2. Expand first, then collect the numbers.","explain":"10 − 2x + 6 = 16 − 2x. 4 − 2x forgets that −2 × (−3) is +6, and 8x − 24 does 10 − 2 first."}],"explain":{"kid":["To expand a bracket, multiply each term inside by the number outside. Do not stop after the first term. −3(x − 4) means −3 × x and −3 × (−4).","Use the sign rules. A negative times a negative is positive. A negative times a positive is negative. So −3 × (−4) = +12, and −3(x − 4) = −3x + 12.","A minus sign on its own means −1. So −(x + 5) = −x − 5. Every sign inside the bracket flips.","Keep the sign with the number in front. In 5 − 2(x + 1), you multiply by −2, not 2. Expand first: 5 − 2x − 2. Then add the numbers: 3 − 2x."],"adult":["Expanding uses the distributive law, a(b + c) = ab + ac, and it holds for negative a, b and c. The multiplier includes its sign. In 10 − 2(x − 3), the bracket is multiplied by −2. That gives −2 × x = −2x and −2 × (−3) = +6, so 10 − 2x + 6 = 16 − 2x.","A minus in front of a bracket is a multiplier of −1, so −(2x − 7) = −2x + 7. This is also why subtracting a whole bracket changes every sign inside it, which you need later for subtracting polynomials.","Order of operations still applies. Multiplication comes before subtraction, so 5 − 2(x + 1) is not 3(x + 1). Expand the bracket first, then collect like terms. The x term and the number term stay separate, so 3 − 2x does not simplify to x.","Common mistakes: multiplying only the first term, as in −3(x − 4) = −3x − 4; getting the second sign wrong, as in −3x − 12; dropping the minus from the multiplier; and subtracting before expanding. To check, substitute a value: with x = 1, 10 − 2(1 − 3) = 14 and 16 − 2 × 1 = 14."]},"familyTip":"Each write down what you think −2(x − 5) expands to, without talking. Then test both answers by putting in x = 3: the original gives −2 × (−2) = 4, so the right expansion must give 4 too. Pick a new bracket and swap who writes first.","keyIdea":"The number in front of a bracket multiplies every term inside it, and its sign goes with it. So −3(x − 4) = −3x + 12, because −3 × −4 = +12. In 5 − 2(x + 1) the multiplier is −2: 5 − 2x − 2 = 3 − 2x.","worked":{"title":"Expand and simplify 10 − 2(x − 3)","steps":["The multiplier is −2, sign included: 10 + (−2)(x − 3).","−2 × x = −2x.","−2 × (−3) = +6, because a negative times a negative is positive.","10 − 2x + 6. Collect the numbers: 16 − 2x."]},"visual":{"type":"plot","caption":"Drag x along the graph of 10 − 2(x − 3). Read any point: the output always equals 16 − 2x, so the two forms are the same expression.","opts":{"mode":"eval","f":"10-2*(x-3)","a":1,"xr":[-2,9],"yr":[-4,22],"step":0.5}},"lab":{"type":"flagexp","caption":"Flagship lab: drag the multiplier, sign and all, onto each term in the bracket. The product and its sign rule appear below; then collect the numbers.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_3BZFJQ69OK":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What does it mean for two expressions to be equivalent?","choices":["They give the same value for every x","They give the same value for one x","They use the same numbers"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about how many values of x have to work.","explain":"Equivalent means equal for every value of the variable. Agreeing once, or sharing numbers, is not enough."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Sam says x + 9 and 3x + 1 are equivalent because both give 13 when x = 4. Is Sam right?","choices":["No: at x = 0 they give 9 and 1","Yes, one match is enough","Yes, because 9 + 4 = 13"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for one value of x where they differ.","explain":"At x = 0, x + 9 = 9 but 3x + 1 = 1. A single counter-example shows they are not equivalent."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"Put x = {a} into 4(x + 1). What value do you get?","answer":"4*a+4","hint":"Add 1 to {a} first, then multiply by 4. It should match 4 × {a} + 4.","explain":"4({a} + 1) = 4 × {=a+1} = {=4*a+4}, the same as 4 × {a} + 4.","params":{"a":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which expression is equivalent to 6x − 2x + 1?","choices":["4x + 1","5x","8x + 1"],"answer":0,"hint":"Collect the x terms. Leave the number term alone.","explain":"6x − 2x = 4x, so the expression is 4x + 1. 5x wrongly joins the 1 to the x terms."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Why is 2x + 3 not equivalent to 5x?","choices":["2x and 3 are unlike terms, so they cannot be added","Because 2 + 3 is not 5","It is equivalent"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can an x term and a plain number be joined into one term?","explain":"2x + 3 is already as simple as it goes. At x = 2 it gives 7, but 5x gives 10."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Testing x = 1, 2 and 3 gives matching values for two expressions. What does this prove?","choices":["Nothing for certain: simplify both to prove it","They are equivalent","They are equivalent for x up to 3"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can a few tests check every possible x?","explain":"Tests can only show a pair is not equivalent. Simplifying both to the same form proves it for every x."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"Simplify 5(x + 2) − 3. What is the number term?","answer":7,"hint":"Expand the bracket first, then combine the two number terms.","explain":"5(x + 2) − 3 = 5x + 10 − 3 = 5x + 7, so the number term is 7."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"3(x + {b}) and 3x + {b} are not equivalent. How much bigger is 3(x + {b}) for every x?","answer":"2*b","hint":"Expand 3(x + {b}) and compare the number terms.","explain":"3(x + {b}) = 3x + {=3*b}, which is {=2*b} more than 3x + {b} for every value of x.","params":{"b":[1,6]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"Put x = {a} into 3(x + 2) and into 3x + 6. What value do you get?","answer":"3*a+6","hint":"Work out the bracket first: {a} + 2, then multiply by 3. Check it against 3 × {a} + 6.","explain":"3({a} + 2) = 3 × {=a+2} = {=3*a+6}, and 3 × {a} + 6 = {=3*a+6}. The same, as it is for every x.","params":{"a":[1,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"2(x + {b}) and 2x + {b} are not equivalent. For any x, how much bigger is 2(x + {b})?","answer":"b","hint":"Expand 2(x + {b}) first, then compare it with 2x + {b}.","explain":"2(x + {b}) = 2x + {=2*b}, which is always {b} more than 2x + {b}. Every x is a counter-example.","params":{"b":[1,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"2x + 4 and x + 6 both give 8 when x = 2. What can you say?","choices":["Nothing yet: test another value or simplify","They are equivalent","They are equivalent for even x"],"answer":0,"hint":"Try x = 5 in both before you decide.","explain":"At x = 5, 2x + 4 = 14 but x + 6 = 11. They only match at x = 2, so they are not equivalent."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Which expression is equivalent to 5(x − 1)?","choices":["5x − 5","5x − 1","4x"],"answer":0,"hint":"Multiply every term in the bracket by 5.","explain":"5 × x = 5x and 5 × (−1) = −5, so 5(x − 1) = 5x − 5. 5x − 1 only multiplied the first term."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p5","prompt":"2x + 3 and 5x agree for exactly one value of x. Which value?","answer":1,"hint":"Set 2x + 3 = 5x and solve, or test small values.","explain":"2x + 3 = 5x gives 3 = 3x, so x = 1. Both give 5 there, but at any other x they differ."}],"explain":{"kid":["3(x + 2) and 3x + 6 look different, but they always give the same answer. Try x = 4: 3 × 6 = 18 and 12 + 6 = 18. We call them equivalent.","To test a pair, put the same number in for x in both. If the answers differ even once, the pair is not equivalent. That one number is a counter-example.","Be careful when the answers match. 2x + 4 and x + 6 both give 8 when x = 2. But at x = 5 they give 14 and 11. A single match can be luck.","To be sure, simplify both. 4x + 2 − x + 1 becomes 3x + 3, and so does 3(x + 1). Same simple form means same value for every x."],"adult":["Equivalent means equal for every value of the variable, not just the ones tried. 2(x + 3) and 2x + 3 are never equal: the first is 2x + 6, so it is always exactly 3 more. Any single x is a counter-example.","Testing has a one-way power. A single different value settles that a pair is not equivalent. No number of matching values can settle that it is, because there are infinitely many x to check.","Two different straight-line expressions can agree at one x at most. That is why 2x + 3 and 5x agree only at x = 1. Proof of equivalence comes from algebra: expand brackets, collect like terms, and compare the simplest forms.","Common mistakes: deciding from one matching value; collecting unlike terms, so 2x + 3 becomes 5x; multiplying only the first term, so 2(x + 3) becomes 2x + 3; and offering a value where the two happen to agree as a counter-example."]},"familyTip":"One person writes two expressions that look different, such as 2(x + 4) and 2x + 8, or a trap pair like x + 5 and 2x + 1. The other picks values of x to test. Can they find a counter-example, or show by simplifying that none exists? Swap roles.","keyIdea":"Two expressions are equivalent when they give the same value for every x. One value where they differ proves they are not. A match at one value proves nothing, so to show they are equivalent, simplify both to the same form.","worked":{"title":"Are 4x + 2 − x + 1 and 3(x + 1) equivalent?","steps":["Test x = 2: 4 × 2 + 2 − 2 + 1 = 9 and 3 × 3 = 9. A match, but not yet a proof.","Simplify the first: collect the x terms, 4x − x = 3x, and the numbers, 2 + 1 = 3. That gives 3x + 3.","Expand the second: 3(x + 1) = 3x + 3.","Both simplify to 3x + 3, so they are equivalent for every x."]},"visual":{"type":"plot","caption":"Drag x along the graph of 3(x + 2). At every point the output equals 3x + 6, which is what equivalent means.","opts":{"mode":"eval","f":"3*(x+2)","a":1,"xr":[-4,6],"yr":[-8,26],"step":0.5}},"lab":{"type":"flagequiv","caption":"Flagship lab: test the same x in two expressions to hunt for a counter-example, or tap the cards to simplify both and prove they match.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_wuDPIpLJwh":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"(x² − {=a*a}) ÷ (x − {a}) simplifies to x + k. What is k?","answer":"a","hint":"Write x² − {=a*a} as two brackets, then see which one matches the bottom.","explain":"x² − {=a*a} = (x − {a})(x + {a}). The (x − {a}) cancels and x + {a} is left, so k = {a}.","params":{"a":[2,8]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"({a}x + {=a*b}) ÷ {a} simplifies to x + k. What is k?","answer":"b","hint":"Take {a} out of both terms on top: {a}x + {=a*b} = {a}(x + ?).","explain":"{a}x + {=a*b} = {a}(x + {b}). The {a} cancels, so the answer is x + {b} and k = {b}.","params":{"a":[2,6],"b":[1,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"(x² − {=a+b}x + {=a*b}) ÷ (x − {a}) simplifies to x − k. What is k?","answer":"b","hint":"Both numbers in the brackets are negative here: they multiply to {=a*b} and add to −{=a+b}.","explain":"x² − {=a+b}x + {=a*b} = (x − {a})(x − {b}). The (x − {a}) cancels, so k = {b}.","params":{"a":[1,4],"b":[5,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Mo writes (x + 6)/6 = x + 1. What went wrong?","choices":["He divided the 6 by 6 but not the x","Nothing, it is right","He should have got x"],"answer":0,"hint":"Dividing the top by 6 means dividing every term on top. Try x = 6 in both.","explain":"(x + 6)/6 = x/6 + 1, not x + 1. At x = 6 the fraction is 12/6 = 2, but x + 1 is 7. It does not simplify."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which of these can be simplified?","choices":["(x² + 3x)/x","(x + 3)/x","(x + 3)/3"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for a fraction where you can factorise the top so it shares a factor with the bottom.","explain":"x² + 3x = x(x + 3), so (x² + 3x)/x = x + 3 with x ≠ 0. In the other two, the x or the 3 is only a term."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Work out 1/x + 2/(x + 1).","choices":["(3x + 1)/(x(x + 1))","3/(2x + 1)","3/(x(x + 1))"],"answer":0,"hint":"Use the common bottom x(x + 1). Multiply the top of each fraction by whatever its bottom is missing.","explain":"1/x = (x + 1)/(x(x + 1)) and 2/(x + 1) = 2x/(x(x + 1)). Add the tops: (3x + 1)/(x(x + 1))."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Simplify (x + 2)/(x − 1) × (x − 1)/(x + 3).","choices":["(x + 2)/(x + 3)","2/3","(x + 2)/(x − 1)"],"answer":0,"hint":"Put both tops over both bottoms. Which bracket is on a top and on a bottom?","explain":"(x − 1) is a factor on the top and on the bottom, so it cancels: (x + 2)/(x + 3). 2/3 comes from crossing out terms."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"(x² + 5x + 6)/(x + 2) = x + 3. For which x is this not true?","choices":["x = −2","x = −3","x = 0"],"answer":0,"hint":"Find where the bottom of the original fraction is 0.","explain":"At x = −2 the original is 0/0, which has no value, while x + 3 gives 1. So write x + 3 with x ≠ −2."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"Dividing by (x + 4)/x is the same as what?","choices":["Multiplying by x/(x + 4)","Multiplying by (x + 4)/x","Taking away (x + 4)/x"],"answer":0,"hint":"To divide by a fraction, turn it upside down and multiply, just like 1 ÷ 3/4.","explain":"Dividing by a fraction means multiplying by its reciprocal, so dividing by (x + 4)/x is multiplying by x/(x + 4)."}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"(x² + {=a+b}x + {=a*b}) ÷ (x + {a}) simplifies to x + k. What is k?","answer":"b","hint":"Factorise the top: find two numbers that multiply to {=a*b} and add to {=a+b}. One of them is {a}.","explain":"x² + {=a+b}x + {=a*b} = (x + {a})(x + {b}). The (x + {a}) cancels, so k = {b}.","params":{"a":[1,4],"b":[2,7]},"where":"a<b"},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Can the x's cancel in (x + 2)/x?","choices":["No, the x on top is a term, not a factor","Yes, it simplifies to 2","Yes, it simplifies to 3"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is the x on top multiplied by something, or added to something? Try x = 4 in the fraction.","explain":"The x is added to 2, so it is a term and cannot cancel. At x = 4 the fraction is 6/4 = 1.5, not 2."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"(x² − {=a*a}) ÷ (x + {a}) simplifies to x − k. What is k?","answer":"a","hint":"x² − {=a*a} is a difference of two squares: (x + {a})(x − {a}).","explain":"x² − {=a*a} = (x + {a})(x − {a}). The (x + {a}) cancels and x − {a} is left, so k = {a}.","params":{"a":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Simplify (3x + 12)/(x² + 4x).","choices":["3/x","3","x/3"],"answer":0,"hint":"Take out a common factor on each side: 3 from the top, x from the bottom. Then look for a shared bracket.","explain":"3(x + 4)/(x(x + 4)): the (x + 4) cancels and 3/x is left. The x on the bottom has nothing to cancel with."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p5","prompt":"(x + {a})/(x − {b}) has no value for one number x. What is it?","answer":"b","hint":"A fraction has no value when its bottom is 0. Solve x − {b} = 0.","explain":"The bottom is 0 when x = {b}, because {b} − {b} = 0. So x = {b} is not allowed.","params":{"a":[1,6],"b":[1,7]}}],"explain":{"kid":["A fraction like 6/8 simplifies because 6 and 8 share a factor of 2. Algebra fractions work the same way. You look for a factor that the top and the bottom share.","So factorise first. x² + 5x + 6 is (x + 2)(x + 3). Now the fraction has (x + 2) on the top and on the bottom. Those cancel, and x + 3 is left.","You can only cancel a whole factor. In (x + 2)/x, the x on top is added to 2. It is a term, not a factor. Crossing out the two x's is wrong. Try x = 4: the fraction is 6/4, not 2.","Watch for numbers that break the fraction. (x² + 5x + 6)/(x + 2) has no value when x = −2, because then the bottom is 0. So the full answer is x + 3, with x not equal to −2."],"adult":["A rational expression simplifies when you divide the top and the bottom by a common factor, just as 6/8 becomes 3/4. Factorise both sides fully first. Take out a common number or letter, then look for a quadratic or a difference of two squares. For example, (2x + 6)/(x² − 9) = 2(x + 3)/((x + 3)(x − 3)) = 2/(x − 3).","The new form equals the old one wherever the old one exists. Any x that made the old bottom zero is still excluded. So (x² + 5x + 6)/(x + 2) = x + 3 holds only for x ≠ −2. Testing one or two values of x is a quick way to check that a step kept the value.","To multiply, put all the tops over all the bottoms, then cancel shared factors. To divide, multiply by the reciprocal. To add or subtract, use a common bottom: 1/x + 2/(x + 1) = (x + 1 + 2x)/(x(x + 1)) = (3x + 1)/(x(x + 1)).","The most common mistake is cancelling terms instead of factors, such as turning (x + 2)/x into 2, or (x + 6)/6 into x + 1. Other slips are dividing only one term of the top, cancelling the wrong bracket, and a sign error while factorising. Be careful: expand your factors and test a value before you cancel."]},"familyTip":"Write a fraction such as (x² − 16)/(x − 4) and ask for its simplest form. Then both pick a number for x, work out the original and the answer, and see if they match. Try x = 4 too, and talk about why the original breaks there.","keyIdea":"Factorise the top and the bottom, then cancel any factor they share. (x² + 5x + 6)/(x + 2) = (x + 2)(x + 3)/(x + 2) = x + 3. Only whole factors cancel, never single terms, and any x that made the original bottom zero is still not allowed.","worked":{"title":"Simplify (x² + x − 6)/(x² − 4)","steps":["Factorise the top: two numbers that multiply to −6 and add to 1 are 3 and −2, so x² + x − 6 = (x + 3)(x − 2).","Factorise the bottom as a difference of two squares: x² − 4 = (x + 2)(x − 2).","(x − 2) is a factor of both, so it cancels: the fraction is (x + 3)/(x + 2).","Check with x = 3: the original is 6/5 and (x + 3)/(x + 2) is 6/5. The original is undefined at x = 2 and x = −2, so x ≠ ±2."]},"visual":{"type":"formula","caption":"Slide x. (x × x − 9) ÷ (x + 3) always works out to the same value as x − 3.","opts":{"name":"value","expr":"(x × x − 9) ÷ (x + 3)","vars":{"x":[0,10,4]}}},"lab":{"type":"flagafrac","caption":"Flagship lab: factorise the top and the bottom into factor cards, then drag a card onto its twin to cancel it. Test any x on the number line.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_tY7a5_hq6j":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which expression is the same as 'increase a by 8%'?","choices":["1.08a","a + 8","1.8a"],"answer":0,"hint":"8% of a is 0.08a. Add it to the whole a.","explain":"a + 0.08a = 1.08a. a + 8 adds £8, not 8%, and 1.8a is an 80% increase."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"What does the 0.65 in 0.65a tell you about a price change?","choices":["65% of the price is left: 35% was taken off","65% was taken off","The price went up 65%"],"answer":0,"hint":"0.65a = a − 0.35a. Which part is left?","explain":"A multiplier below 1 is a decrease. 0.65a means 65% is left, so the price fell by 35%."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"A £{a} ticket rises by 20%. What is the new price in pounds?","answer":"a*6/5","hint":"a + 0.2a = 1.2a. Multiply {a} by 1.2.","explain":"{a} × 1.2 = {=a*6/5}, the same as {a} + {=a/5}.","params":{"a":[10,60]},"where":"a%5==0"},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which form shows that 4n + 8 is always a multiple of 4?","choices":["4(n + 2)","2(2n + 4)","8 + 4n"],"answer":0,"hint":"Take out the whole common factor of 4n and 8.","explain":"4n + 8 = 4(n + 2), 4 lots of a whole number. 2(2n + 4) takes out only a 2, and 8 + 4n just swaps the order."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"A price goes up 10%, then the new price goes down 10%. What is the final price?","choices":["0.99a, a little lower than at the start","a, the same as at the start","1.01a, a little higher"],"answer":0,"hint":"Multiply the two multipliers, 1.1 and 0.9.","explain":"1.1 × 0.9 = 0.99. The 10% off is taken from the bigger price, so more comes off than was added."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"Shop A sells a £50 coat at 30% off. What is the sale price in pounds?","answer":35,"hint":"30% off leaves 70%, so multiply 50 by 0.7.","explain":"a − 0.3a = 0.7a, and 0.7 × 50 = 35. Multiplying by 0.3 would give the £15 discount, not the price."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Each ticket costs £12 plus a £3 fee per ticket. Which form shows the cost of one ticket most clearly?","choices":["15n","12n + 3n","12n + 3"],"answer":0,"hint":"Collect the n terms: 12n + 3n.","explain":"12n + 3n = 15n, so each ticket costs £15. 12n + 3 would charge the £3 fee only once."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"A price rises by {p}%. What single multiplier, as a decimal, gives the new price?","answer":"(100+p)/100","hint":"Keep the whole price, 1, and add {p}% of it as a decimal.","explain":"a + {=p/100}a = {=(100+p)/100}a, so the multiplier is {=(100+p)/100}.","params":{"p":[5,45]},"where":"p%5==0"}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"A £{a} bike goes up by 10%. Use a × 1.1 to find the new price in pounds.","answer":"a*11/10","hint":"a + 0.1a = 1.1a. Multiply {a} by 1.1.","explain":"{a} × 1.1 = {=a*11/10}. That is the same as {a} + {=a/10}, the price plus 10% of it.","params":{"a":[20,90]},"where":"a%10==0"},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A price is cut by {p}%. What single multiplier, as a decimal, gives the new price?","answer":"(100-p)/100","hint":"a − {p}% of a leaves (100 − {p})% of a. Write that as a decimal.","explain":"a − {=p/100}a = {=(100-p)/100}a, so you multiply by {=(100-p)/100}. Multiplying by {=p/100} would give only the part taken off.","params":{"p":[10,40]},"where":"p%5==0"},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which form shows that 6n + 9 is a multiple of 3 for every whole number n?","choices":["3(2n + 3)","9 + 6n","6(n + 9)"],"answer":0,"hint":"Find the largest number that divides both 6n and 9, and take it out.","explain":"3 divides 6n and 9, so 6n + 9 = 3(2n + 3): 3 lots of a whole number. 9 + 6n only swaps the order, and 6(n + 9) = 6n + 54."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"A price has 20% off, then 10% off the new price. What is the overall change?","choices":["28% off","30% off","10% off"],"answer":0,"hint":"Write each change as a multiplier, then multiply them.","explain":"0.8 × 0.9 = 0.72, and 0.72a = a − 0.28a. The second 10% comes off a smaller price, so the total is 28%, not 30%."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p5","prompt":"Savings of £{a} earn 5% interest for one year. Use 1.05a to find the new total in pounds.","answer":"a*21/20","hint":"a + 0.05a = 1.05a. Multiply {a} by 1.05.","explain":"{a} × 1.05 = {=a*21/20}: the £{a} you had plus {=a/20} of interest.","params":{"a":[20,100]},"where":"a%20==0"}],"explain":{"kid":["A game costs a pounds and the price goes up 5%. You can write the new price as a + 0.05a. That shows the old price plus the extra bit.","Collect the a terms: 1a + 0.05a = 1.05a. Now one number, 1.05, does the whole job. To go up 5%, multiply by 1.05.","Going down works the same way. Take 15% off and a − 0.15a = 0.85a. The 0.85 tells you that 85% of the price is left.","Factors can show a pattern too. 6n + 9 = 3(2n + 3). The second form is 3 lots of a whole number, so it is always in the 3 times table."],"adult":["Rewriting changes the form of an expression, not its value. Each form makes a different fact easy to read: a + 0.2a shows the increase as a part of a, 1.2a shows the multiplier, and 3(2n + 3) shows a factor of 3.","The multiplier form is the one that scales. Two changes in a row multiply: 20% off then 10% off is 0.8 × 0.9 = 0.72, so 28% off, not 30%. Repeated growth becomes powers, as in 1.05ⁿ for compound interest.","Choosing the form is part of the answer. To compare two offers, write each as a single multiplier. To show a total is always even or a multiple of 3, factor it.","Common mistakes: writing a + 5 for a 5% rise; multiplying by 0.15 for a 15% decrease, which gives the part removed, not what is left; writing 1.5 for 5%; adding percentages for changes in a row; and taking out only part of a common factor."]},"familyTip":"Look at a shop receipt or a sale sign together. If something is 20% off, what single number do you multiply the price by? Check with a calculator. Then ask: is 20% off and then another 10% off the same as 30% off?","keyIdea":"Equivalent forms of an expression give the same value but show different things. a + 0.05a shows the 5% added; 1.05a shows one multiplier that does the whole change. Pick the form that answers the question you were asked.","worked":{"title":"A £40 jacket is 20% off, then the sale price drops a further 10%. What single change is that?","steps":["20% off leaves 80%, so the first price is 0.8a. For a = £40 that is £32.","10% off the new price leaves 90% of it: 0.9 × 0.8a = 0.72a. For a = £40 that is £28.80.","0.72a = a − 0.28a, so the two discounts together are 28% off.","Check: 28% of £40 is £11.20, and £40 − £11.20 = £28.80. It is not 30% off, which would be £28."]},"visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Each form tells you something different. Sort each expression by what it shows. Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["One multiplier","A common factor","The part added or taken"],"items":[["1.05a",0],["0.85a",0],["3(2n + 3)",1],["4(n + 2)",1],["a + 0.05a",2],["a − 0.15a",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagreveal","caption":"Flagship lab: stretch and shrink a price bar to turn a + 0.25a into 1.25a, match two discounts with one, and regroup 6n + 9 dots into 3 equal rows.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_mSGzqulVLx":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which surd is already in simplest form?","choices":["√18","√30","√20"],"answer":1,"hint":"Does 4, 9, 16 or 25 divide the number?","explain":"30 = 2 × 3 × 5 has no square factor. 18 = 9 × 2 and 20 = 4 × 5 both do, so they simplify to 3√2 and 2√5."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"Simplify √{=k*k*m}. It equals a√{m}; type a.","answer":"k","hint":"Try dividing by {m}, then look for a square number.","explain":"{=k*k*m} ÷ {m} = {=k*k} = {k}², so √{=k*k*m} = {k}√{m}.","params":{"k":[2,7],"m":[5,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Ella writes √12 + √3 = √15. What is correct?","choices":["4√3","√15 is right","2√3 + √3 = 3√3"],"answer":2,"hint":"Simplify √12 first. Roots do not add inside.","explain":"√12 = 2√3, so √12 + √3 = 2√3 + √3 = 3√3 ≈ 5.20. √15 ≈ 3.87, a different number."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"Rationalise {=t*m}/√{m}. It equals c√{m}; type c.","answer":"t","hint":"Multiply top and bottom by √{m}. The bottom becomes {m}.","explain":"{=t*m}/√{m} × √{m}/√{m} = {=t*m}√{m} ÷ {m} = {t}√{m}, so c = {t}.","params":{"t":[2,6],"m":[2,3]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"Work out √3 × √12.","answer":6,"hint":"Multiply under one root first: √(3 × 12).","explain":"√3 × √12 = √(3 × 12) = √36 = 6, a whole number, because 36 is a square."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Write 3√5 as a single surd.","choices":["√15","√35","√45"],"answer":2,"hint":"The 3 goes back under the root as 3².","explain":"3 = √9, so 3√5 = √9 × √5 = √45. √15 comes from putting the 3 in without squaring it."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Which is 1/√3 written with a rational denominator?","choices":["√3/3","1/3","3√3"],"answer":0,"hint":"Multiply top and bottom by √3.","explain":"1/√3 × √3/√3 = √3/3 ≈ 0.58. 1/3 drops the root, and 3√3 is about 5.2."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Why is 3√8 not the simplest form of √72?","choices":["3 is not a square number","8 still has the square factor 4","A surd can never have a number in front"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the number left under the root.","explain":"8 = 4 × 2, so 3√8 = 3 × 2√2 = 6√2. Using the biggest square, 36, gets there in one step."}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"√{=k*k*m} = a√{m}. Find a.","answer":"k","hint":"Divide {=k*k*m} by {m}. The result is a square number: take its root.","explain":"{=k*k*m} = {=k*k} × {m} and √{=k*k} = {k}, so √{=k*k*m} = {k}√{m} and a = {k}.","params":{"k":[2,9],"m":[2,3]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Work out √{a} × √{=a*k*k}.","answer":"a*k","hint":"√x × √y = √(x × y). Multiply first, then take the root.","explain":"√{a} × √{=a*k*k} = √{=a*a*k*k} = {=a*k}, because {=a*k}² = {=a*a*k*k}.","params":{"a":[2,7],"k":[2,5]},"where":"a!=4"},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which is √72 in simplest form?","choices":["3√8","36√2","6√2"],"answer":2,"hint":"Find the biggest square number that divides 72.","explain":"72 = 36 × 2, so √72 = 6√2. 3√8 is not finished because 8 = 4 × 2, and 36√2 forgot to root the 36."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"√{=a*a*m} + √{=b*b*m} = c√{m}. Find c.","answer":"a+b","hint":"Simplify each surd first. Both should end in √{m}.","explain":"√{=a*a*m} = {a}√{m} and √{=b*b*m} = {b}√{m}, so the sum is {=a+b}√{m} and c = {=a+b}.","params":{"a":[2,4],"b":[3,5],"m":[2,3]},"where":"a<b"},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Rationalise 1/√2.","choices":["1/2","√2/2","√2"],"answer":1,"hint":"Multiply the top and the bottom by √2.","explain":"1/√2 × √2/√2 = √2/2 ≈ 0.71. Both are the same number; √2/2 just has a whole-number bottom."}],"explain":{"kid":["√12 means the side of a square with area 12. It is not a whole number.","Cut that square into 4 equal squares. Each has area 3, so each side is √3. Two of them make the big side, so √12 = 2√3.","Look for square numbers inside: 4, 9, 16, 25, 36. 50 = 25 × 2, and √25 = 5, so √50 = 5√2.","Surds with the same root add like apples: 2√3 + 5√3 = 7√3."],"adult":["Because √(ab) = √a × √b for a, b ≥ 0, write the number as a square times the rest and root the square: √72 = √(36 × 2) = 6√2. A surd is in simplest form when the number under the root has no square factor above 1.","Use the largest square factor, or keep going until none is left: √72 = √(9 × 8) = 3√8 = 3 × 2√2 = 6√2. The rule also runs backwards: √2 × √8 = √16 = 4, and 3√5 = √(9 × 5) = √45.","Only like surds combine when adding: √12 + √27 = 2√3 + 3√3 = 5√3. To rationalise a denominator, multiply top and bottom by the surd: 6/√3 = 6√3/3 = 2√3, and 1/√2 = √2/2.","Common mistakes: taking the square out without rooting it (√50 = 25√2); stopping at a factor that still holds a square (3√8); writing √a + √b = √(a + b); adding the numbers under like roots (5√6 for 5√3); and not dividing by the bottom when rationalising."]},"familyTip":"Draw a square on squared paper with area 8 by tilting it (corners 2 across and 2 up). Then build a 2 × 2 grid of those tilted squares. Its area is 32 and its side is 2√8 = 4√2. Measure both sides with a ruler.","keyIdea":"√(a × b) = √a × √b, so a square factor can come out of the root: √72 = √36 × √2 = 6√2. Take out the biggest square factor. Like surds add like like terms, and 1/√2 = √2/2 after multiplying top and bottom by √2.","worked":{"title":"Simplify √72, then √72 + √8 and 6/√2.","steps":["Square factors of 72: 4, 9, 36. The biggest is 36, and 72 = 36 × 2.","√72 = √36 × √2 = 6√2.","√8 = √(4 × 2) = 2√2, so √72 + √8 = 6√2 + 2√2 = 8√2.","6/√2 × √2/√2 = 6√2/2 = 3√2. Check: 3√2 ≈ 4.24 and 6 ÷ 1.414 ≈ 4.24."]},"visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Is each surd in simplest form? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Simplest form","Can be simplified"],"items":[["3√2",0],["5√7",0],["√30",0],["√18",1],["2√12",1],["3√8",1],["√50",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagsurd","caption":"Flagship lab: slice the square with side √n into a grid of equal squares to see √n = k√m, then build a surd back up and rationalise 1/√2.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_sj3itZ2sso":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"A plant is {a} mm tall and grows by 5% each week: {a} × 1.05ᵗ. How tall is it at t = 0?","answer":"a","hint":"Any number to the power 0 is 1.","explain":"1.05⁰ = 1, so at t = 0 the height is {a} × 1 = {a} mm. That is the start value.","params":{"a":[20,90]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"Each year a value is multiplied by {=1-r/100}. By what percentage does it fall each year?","answer":"r","hint":"Turn {=1-r/100} into a percentage, then compare it with 100%.","explain":"{=1-r/100} is {=100-r}% of the year before. 100% − {=100-r}% = {r}%, so it falls by {r}% a year.","params":{"r":[5,40]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"Work out 200 × 1.1².","answer":242,"hint":"1.1² = 1.1 × 1.1. Do the power before you multiply by 200.","explain":"1.1² = 1.21 and 200 × 1.21 = 242. Adding 10% of 200 twice gives 240, which misses the 10% of the first year’s 20."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Ben says a 15% decrease each year means multiplying by 0.15. What is right?","choices":["Ben is right","Multiply by 0.85, the part that is left","Multiply by 1.15"],"answer":1,"hint":"After losing 15%, how much of the amount is still there?","explain":"Losing 15% leaves 85%, so the factor is 0.85. Multiplying by 0.15 would keep only the 15% that was lost."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"A phone costs 800 and loses 25% of its value each year. What is it worth after 2 years?","answer":450,"hint":"The factor is 0.75. Multiply 800 by 0.75 twice.","explain":"800 × 0.75 = 600, then 600 × 0.75 = 450. So 800 × 0.75² = 450. Taking off 200 twice would give 400, which is wrong."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which story fits 60 × 1.5ᵗ?","choices":["60 rabbits, and the number goes up by half each year","60 rabbits, and 1.5 more rabbits each year","1.5 rabbits, and the number goes up by 60% each year"],"answer":0,"hint":"60 is the start. 1.5 = 150% of the year before.","explain":"a = 60 is the start and b = 1.5 means 150% of last year: a rise of 50%, or half, each year."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"In 1200 × 0.9ᵗ, which statement is true?","choices":["It starts at 1080","It starts at 1200 and falls by 10% each step","It starts at 1200 and falls by 90% each step"],"answer":1,"hint":"At t = 0, 0.9⁰ = 1. Then compare 0.9 with 1.","explain":"The start value is 1200 and 0.9 = 90% of the step before, so it falls by 10% each step. 1080 is the value at t = 1."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which factor means the amount stays the same every year?","choices":["b = 0","b = 1","b = 100"],"answer":1,"hint":"What do you multiply a number by to leave it unchanged?","explain":"Multiplying by 1 changes nothing, so a × 1ᵗ = a for every t. A factor above 1 grows and a factor between 0 and 1 shrinks."}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"Money in a savings account grows by {r}% each year. What is the growth factor b?","answer":"1+r/100","hint":"Keep 100% of the money and add {r}%. Turn that total percentage into a decimal.","explain":"100% + {r}% = {=100+r}%, and {=100+r}% as a decimal is {=1+r/100}. So b = {=1+r/100}.","params":{"r":[2,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A town’s population falls by {r}% each year. What is the decay factor b?","answer":"1-r/100","hint":"Work out the percentage that is left after the fall, then write it as a decimal.","explain":"100% − {r}% = {=100-r}% is left each year, so b = {=1-r/100}. Using {=r/100} would keep only the part that was lost.","params":{"r":[10,40]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A colony starts with {a} cells and doubles every hour, so it has {a} × 2ᵗ cells after t hours. How many cells are there after {t} hours?","answer":"a*2**t","hint":"Work out 2 to the power {t} first: multiply 2 by itself {t} times. Then multiply by {a}.","explain":"2 to the power {t} is {=2**t}, so {a} × {=2**t} = {=a*2**t} cells. Doubling {t} times multiplies the start by {=2**t}.","params":{"a":[3,12],"t":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"A savings balance is 500 × 1.04ᵗ after t years. What does the 1.04 tell you?","choices":["The balance grows by 4% each year","The balance grows by 1.04 each year","The balance starts at 1.04"],"answer":0,"hint":"1.04 = 104% of the year before. Compare that with 100%.","explain":"1.04 = 100% + 4%, so each year the balance is multiplied by 1.04: it grows by 4%. The start value is 500."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"A pond has 80 fish, and the number falls by 15% each year. Which expression gives the fish after t years?","choices":["80 × 0.15ᵗ","80 × 0.85ᵗ","80 × 1.15ᵗ"],"answer":1,"hint":"What percentage of the fish is left after one year?","explain":"85% of the fish are left each year, so b = 0.85 and the expression is 80 × 0.85ᵗ. 0.15 keeps only the lost part, and 1.15 is a rise."}],"explain":{"kid":["You put 200 in a bank. Each year the bank adds 5% of what is there. The amount is 200 × 1.05ᵗ after t years.","The 200 is where you start. At t = 0 no years have passed, so you still have 200.","The 1.05 is what you times by each year. You keep all of it, 100%, and get 5% more. That is 105%, or 1.05. You times the new amount each time, so 200 grows by 10, then by 10.50, then by a bit more.","If a thing loses 15% a year, 85% is left. So you times by 0.85 each year, not by 0.15."],"adult":["An expression a × bᵗ describes a quantity that is multiplied by the same factor b in every time step. At t = 0, b⁰ = 1, so the value is a: the start value. In 500 × 1.04ᵗ the account opens with 500.","The factor compares each step with the one before. A rise of r% keeps the whole amount and adds r%, so b = 1 + r/100 (6% gives 1.06). A fall of r% leaves 100% − r%, so b = 1 − r/100 (15% off gives 0.85). Read backwards, b = 0.92 means a fall of 8% and b = 1.3 a rise of 30%.","Because each percentage is taken of the new amount, the change itself grows or shrinks. 200 × 1.1ᵗ gives 220, 242, 266.2: it doubles after about 7.3 years, while adding a flat 20 a year takes 10. With 0 < b < 1 the losses get smaller each step and the value never reaches 0.","Common mistakes: using r/100 as the factor (0.15 for a 15% fall, which keeps only the lost part); writing 1.6 for 6%; reading the value at t = 1 as the start; working out (a × b)ᵗ instead of a × bᵗ; and adding the first year's change every year, as if the growth were linear."]},"familyTip":"Fold a sheet of paper in half again and again and count the layers: 1, 2, 4, 8, 16. That is 1 × 2ᵗ. Then guess how many layers 7 folds would make (128) before you try it.","keyIdea":"In a × bᵗ, a is the start value (the value at t = 0) and b is the factor you multiply by each step. A rise of r% gives b = 1 + r/100, a fall of r% gives b = 1 − r/100: +4% is × 1.04 and −15% is × 0.85.","worked":{"title":"A car is worth 12000 and loses 20% of its value each year. Write its value after t years and find it after 2 years.","steps":["Start value: a = 12000, the value now, at t = 0.","Losing 20% leaves 100% − 20% = 80%, so b = 0.8.","Value after t years: 12000 × 0.8ᵗ.","After 2 years: 0.8² = 0.64 and 12000 × 0.64 = 7680. Check: 12000 × 0.8 = 9600, then 9600 × 0.8 = 7680."]},"visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Does each change make the amount grow or shrink? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Grows","Shrinks"],"items":[["× 1.04 each year",0],["× 2 each hour",0],["+15% each year",0],["× 0.85 each year",1],["× 0.5 each day",1],["−3% each month",1],["× 0.99 each step",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flaggrow","caption":"Flagship lab: every bar is the one before times b. Set the start value and the factor, turn percentages into factors, and see why multiplying beats adding.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_Is46rra0RY":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"y = {a} × 3ˣ. Find y when x = {n}.","answer":"a*3**n","hint":"Work out 3 to the power {n} first, then multiply by {a}.","explain":"3 to the power {n} is {=3**n}, so y = {a} × {=3**n} = {=a*3**n}.","params":{"a":[1,4],"n":[1,3]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"y = {a} × 0.5ˣ. Find y when x = −{n}.","answer":"a*2**n","hint":"Each step left on this curve divides by 0.5, which doubles y.","explain":"0.5 to the power −{n} is 2 to the power {n} = {=2**n}, so y = {a} × {=2**n} = {=a*2**n}. A decay curve gets bigger to the left.","params":{"a":[1,5],"n":[1,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which curve crosses the y-axis at (0, 4)?","choices":["y = 2 × 4ˣ","y = 4 × 2ˣ","y = 4ˣ"],"answer":1,"hint":"Put x = 0 into each one. Any number to the power 0 is 1.","explain":"At x = 0, 4 × 2⁰ = 4 × 1 = 4. The others give 2 and 1: the y-intercept is a, not b."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Amir says y = 2 × 3ˣ reaches 0 if you go far enough left. Is he right?","choices":["Yes, at about x = −5","No: y is always positive, so it only gets close to 0","Yes, it crosses below 0"],"answer":1,"hint":"What is 2 × 3⁻⁵? Is it positive?","explain":"2 × 3⁻⁵ = 2/243 ≈ 0.008, still positive. Each step left divides by 3, so y approaches 0 but never gets there."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"How are the graphs of y = 3 × 2ˣ and y = 3 × 0.5ˣ related?","choices":["Reflections of each other in the x-axis","Reflections of each other in the y-axis","The same graph"],"answer":1,"hint":"0.5ˣ = (1/2)ˣ = 2⁻ˣ. What does swapping x for −x do to a graph?","explain":"3 × 0.5ˣ = 3 × 2⁻ˣ, so each point (x, y) on one is (−x, y) on the other: a reflection in the y-axis. Both stay above the x-axis."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"A decay curve passes through (0, 6) and (1, 3). Which equation fits?","choices":["y = 6 × 0.5ˣ","y = 3 × 0.5ˣ","y = 6 − 3x"],"answer":0,"hint":"a is the y-value at x = 0. b is y at x = 1 divided by a.","explain":"a = 6 and b = 3 ÷ 6 = 0.5, so y = 6 × 0.5ˣ. y = 6 − 3x also fits two points but is a straight line that hits 0 at x = 2."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"For y = 4 × bˣ, which value of b makes the graph go down from left to right?","choices":["b = 3","b = 0.7","b = 1"],"answer":1,"hint":"Compare each b with 1, not with 4.","explain":"0.7 is between 0 and 1, so y is multiplied by 0.7 at each step right: decay. b = 3 grows, and b = 1 gives the flat line y = 4."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"y = 5 × 2ˣ. Find y when x = 3.","answer":40,"hint":"2³ = 2 × 2 × 2. Do the power before multiplying by 5.","explain":"2³ = 8, so y = 5 × 8 = 40. Working out (5 × 2)³ = 1000 puts the power on the 5 as well."}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"Where does y = {a} × {b}ˣ cross the y-axis? Type the y-value.","answer":"a","hint":"On the y-axis x = 0. Work out {b} to the power 0 first.","explain":"At x = 0, {b} to the power 0 is 1, so y = {a} × 1 = {a}. The curve crosses at (0, {a}).","params":{"a":[2,9],"b":[2,5]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"y = {a} × 2ˣ. Find y when x = −1.","answer":"a/2","hint":"2 to the power −1 is 1 over 2, not −2. Going one step left halves y.","explain":"2⁻¹ = 1/2, so y = {a} × 1/2 = {=a/2}. The value is still positive: the curve stays above the x-axis.","params":{"a":[3,19]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"What does the graph of y = 5 × 0.6ˣ do as x increases?","choices":["It rises more and more steeply","It falls towards 0 but never reaches it","It falls and crosses the x-axis"],"answer":1,"hint":"Compare b = 0.6 with 1. Can 5 × 0.6ˣ ever be 0 or negative?","explain":"0.6 is between 0 and 1, so each step right multiplies y by 0.6: decay. A positive number times 0.6 stays positive, so it never reaches 0."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Which line does y = 2 × 3ˣ get closer and closer to as x gets very negative?","choices":["y = 0","y = 2","x = 0"],"answer":0,"hint":"Work out y at x = −1, −2, −3 and look at the pattern.","explain":"y = 2/3, 2/9, 2/27, … each a third of the last. The values approach 0 without reaching it, so y = 0 is the asymptote. The curve crosses y = 2 at x = 0."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p5","prompt":"The curve y = a × bˣ passes through (0, {a}) and (1, {=a*b}). Find b.","answer":"b","hint":"(0, {a}) tells you a. At x = 1, y = a × b.","explain":"a = {a} from the y-intercept. At x = 1, y = {a} × b = {=a*b}, so b = {=a*b} ÷ {a} = {b}.","params":{"a":[2,6],"b":[2,5]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Draw y = 3 × 2ˣ. At x = 0 you get 3 × 1 = 3, so the curve meets the y-axis at 3.","Each step to the right doubles y: 3, 6, 12, 24. The gaps get bigger, so the curve bends up and gets steep.","Each step to the left halves y: 1.5, then 0.75, then 0.375. It gets tiny, but half of a tiny number is still more than 0. So the curve never hits the x-axis.","Now try y = 3 × 0.5ˣ. It is the same curve flipped left to right. It starts at 3 and falls towards 0 as you go right."],"adult":["For y = a × bˣ with a > 0 and b > 0, the y-intercept is (0, a) because b⁰ = 1. The point (1, a × b) fixes the factor, so two points such as (0, 4) and (1, 6) give a = 4 and b = 1.5.","If b > 1 the graph shows growth: each unit step right multiplies y by b, so the rises get larger and the curve steepens. If 0 < b < 1 it shows decay towards 0. y = (1/b)ˣ is the reflection of y = bˣ in the y-axis, since (1/b)ˣ = b⁻ˣ.","Because every value is a positive number times a positive power, y is always above 0. Moving left on a growth curve (or right on a decay curve) divides by b again and again, so y approaches 0 without reaching it: the x-axis, y = 0, is a horizontal asymptote.","Common mistakes: reading the y-intercept as b or as a × b; joining plotted points with straight lines or letting the curve cross the x-axis; thinking b < a means decay (compare b with 1); treating 2⁻¹ as −2 rather than 1/2; and mistaking a decay curve for a straight line that hits zero."]},"familyTip":"Fill a glass with water and pour away half, then half of what is left, again and again. Mark the level each time on a piece of tape. The marks get closer together but the glass is never empty in theory: that is the curve 1 × 0.5ˣ and its asymptote.","keyIdea":"The graph of y = a × bˣ (a > 0) crosses the y-axis at (0, a). If b > 1 it rises ever more steeply (growth); if 0 < b < 1 it falls towards 0 (decay). It gets closer and closer to y = 0 but never touches it: y = 0 is a horizontal asymptote.","worked":{"title":"Sketch y = 3 × 2ˣ and y = 3 × 0.5ˣ from x = −2 to x = 2, and compare them.","steps":["y = 3 × 2ˣ: x = −2, −1, 0, 1, 2 gives y = 0.75, 1.5, 3, 6, 12.","y = 3 × 0.5ˣ: the same x values give y = 12, 6, 3, 1.5, 0.75.","Both pass through (0, 3) because a = 3. The first rises (b = 2 > 1); the second falls (b = 0.5 < 1).","Each is the other reflected in the y-axis, and both get close to y = 0 on one side without touching it. Draw smooth curves, not straight segments."]},"visual":{"type":"plot","caption":"Drag x along y = 3 × 2ˣ. Each step of 1 to the right doubles y. To the left, y halves each step but stays above 0.","opts":{"mode":"eval","f":"3*2**x","a":0,"xr":[-4,3],"yr":[-2,26],"step":0.5}},"lab":{"type":"flagexpg","caption":"Flagship lab: drag the y-intercept and the point above x = 1 to set a and b, walk a probe along the curve, zoom in near y = 0, and mirror a curve in the y-axis.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_ddCyW84sRo":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"x − 4 = 6. What is x?","answer":10,"hint":"Add 4 to both sides.","explain":"x = 6 + 4 = 10."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"3x + 2 = 14. What is x?","answer":4,"hint":"Subtract 2 from both sides, then divide by 3.","explain":"3x = 12, so x = 4. Check: 3 × 4 + 2 = 14."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which step keeps 5 + x = 9 balanced?","choices":["Take 5 from both sides","Take 5 from the left side only","Add 5 to the left and take 5 from the right"],"answer":0,"hint":"Both sides must change in the same way.","explain":"Taking 5 from both sides gives x = 4, and the equation stays balanced."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"x + {a} = {=a+b}. What is x?","answer":"b","hint":"Take {a} from both sides.","explain":"x = {=a+b} − {a} = {b}.","params":{"a":[2,9],"b":[3,15]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"{a}x = {=a*b}. What is x?","answer":"b","hint":"Divide both sides by {a}.","explain":"x = {=a*b} ÷ {a} = {b}.","params":{"a":[2,6],"b":[2,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"{a}x + {b} = {=a*c+b}. What is x?","answer":"c","hint":"Take {b} from both sides first.","explain":"Subtract {b}: {a}x = {=a*c}. Divide by {a}: x = {c}.","params":{"a":[2,5],"b":[1,9],"c":[1,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"x − {a} = {b}. What is x?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Add {a} to both sides.","explain":"x = {b} + {a} = {=a+b}.","params":{"a":[2,9],"b":[1,9]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"x + {a} = {=a+b}. Find x.","answer":"b","hint":"Take {a} from both sides.","explain":"x = {=a+b} − {a} = {b}.","params":{"a":[2,6],"b":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"What is a good first step to solve x + 5 = 12?","choices":["Subtract 5 from both sides","Add 5 to both sides","Subtract 5 from the left side only"],"answer":0,"hint":"You want x on its own, and you must keep the balance.","explain":"Subtracting 5 from both sides gives x = 7 and keeps the equation balanced."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"2x = 10. What is x?","answer":5,"hint":"Divide both sides by 2.","explain":"x = 10 ÷ 2 = 5."},{"type":"order","id":"p4","prompt":"Put the steps in order to solve 2x + 1 = 9.","items":["2x + 1 = 9","2x = 8","x = 4","Check: 2 × 4 + 1 = 9"],"hint":"First undo the + 1, then undo the × 2.","explain":"Subtract 1 from both sides, divide both sides by 2, then check."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p5","prompt":"{a}x = {=a*b}. Find x.","answer":"b","hint":"Divide both sides by {a}.","explain":"x = {=a*b} ÷ {a} = {b}.","params":{"a":[2,5],"b":[2,6]}}],"explain":{"kid":["An equation is like balanced scales: both sides weigh the same. If x + 3 = 7, take 3 off both sides to keep it balanced, and you get x = 4.","The golden rule: whatever you do to one side, do to the other."],"adult":["An equation says two expressions are equal. Doing the same operation to both sides (adding, subtracting, multiplying, or dividing by a non-zero number) keeps them equal, so each step gives an equivalent equation.","Example: 2x + 1 = 9. Subtract 1 from both sides: 2x = 8. Divide both sides by 2: x = 4. Check by substituting: 2 × 4 + 1 = 9."]},"familyTip":"Play 'mystery bag': a bag with some counters plus 3 loose counters balances 7 counters. How many are in the bag?","keyIdea":"Do the same thing to both sides and the equation stays balanced.","visual":{"type":"balance","caption":"Keep the scale level: do the same thing to both sides until x is alone.","opts":{"mode":"solve","a":1,"b":3,"c":7}}},"mt_MbTdcPBHh-":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Expand (x − 5)².","choices":["x² − 25","x² − 10x + 25","x² − 10x − 25"],"answer":1,"hint":"Write it as (x − 5)(x − 5) and multiply every part.","explain":"x² − 5x − 5x + 25 = x² − 10x + 25. The corner is (−5) × (−5) = +25, and x² − 25 drops the middle term."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"(x + {n})² = x² + bx + c. What is c?","answer":"n*n","hint":"c is the corner square, {n} by {n}.","explain":"c = {n} × {n} = {=n*n}. Not {=2*n}: that is the middle coefficient b.","params":{"n":[2,12]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Factorise x² + 25.","choices":["(x + 5)(x − 5)","It cannot be factorised with real numbers","(x + 5)²"],"answer":1,"hint":"Expand each option and compare with x² + 25.","explain":"(x + 5)(x − 5) = x² − 25 and (x + 5)² = x² + 10x + 25. A sum of two squares has no real factors."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"Work out 52 × 48 without a calculator.","answer":2496,"tolerance":0.001,"hint":"Write it as (50 + 2)(50 − 2).","explain":"This is a difference of squares: 52 × 48 = 50² − 2² = 2500 − 4 = 2496."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Sam writes (x + 4)² = x² + 16. What went wrong?","choices":["Nothing, it is correct","He should have written x² + 4²","He dropped the two strips: the middle term 8x is missing"],"answer":2,"hint":"Draw the square of side x + 4. How many pieces does it have?","explain":"(x + 4)² = x² + 4x + 4x + 16 = x² + 8x + 16. Squaring each part alone leaves out the two x by 4 strips."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"x² − k = (x + {n})(x − {n}). What is k?","answer":"n*n","hint":"Expand the brackets. The x terms cancel.","explain":"(x + {n})(x − {n}) = x² − {n}x + {n}x − {=n*n} = x² − {=n*n}, so k = {=n*n}.","params":{"n":[2,15]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Which is equal to (x + 6)(x − 6)?","choices":["x² − 12x − 36","x² − 36","x² + 36"],"answer":1,"hint":"Multiply out. What happens to +6x and −6x?","explain":"x² − 6x + 6x − 36 = x² − 36. The middle terms cancel, and +6 × −6 is −36."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Factorise x² − 14x + 49.","choices":["(x − 7)²","(x + 7)(x − 7)","(x − 14)²"],"answer":0,"hint":"49 = 7², and the middle term is negative.","explain":"x² − 14x + 49 = (x − 7)², since (−7)² = 49 and 2 × (−7) = −14. (x + 7)(x − 7) has no middle term."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m9","prompt":"Use the difference of two squares to work out 101² − 99².","answer":400,"tolerance":0.001,"hint":"a² − b² = (a + b)(a − b).","explain":"Use the difference of two squares: 101² − 99² = (101 + 99)(101 − 99) = 200 × 2 = 400."},{"type":"mc","id":"m10","prompt":"Factorise 16x² − 1.","choices":["(16x + 1)(16x − 1)","(8x + 1)(8x − 1)","(4x + 1)(4x − 1)"],"answer":2,"hint":"Which term squares to 16x²? And 1 = 1².","explain":"16x² − 1 = (4x)² − 1² = (4x + 1)(4x − 1). 8x squared is 64x², and 16x squared is 256x²."}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"Expand (x + {n})². What is the coefficient of x?","answer":"2*n","hint":"There are two strips, each {n} by x. Add them.","explain":"(x + {n})² = x² + {n}x + {n}x + {=n*n} = x² + {=2*n}x + {=n*n}, so the coefficient is {=2*n}.","params":{"n":[2,12]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Without a calculator, work out ({a} + 3) × ({a} − 3).","answer":"a*a-9","hint":"This is a difference of two squares: ({a} + 3)({a} − 3) = {a}² − 3².","explain":"{a}² − 3² = {=a*a} − 9 = {=a*a-9}. The 3 by 3 corner is taken away.","params":{"a":[20,90]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Factorise x² − 49.","choices":["(x − 7)²","(x + 7)(x − 7)","(x + 49)(x − 49)"],"answer":1,"hint":"49 = 7². Use a² − b² = (a + b)(a − b).","explain":"x² − 49 = (x + 7)(x − 7). (x − 7)² gives x² − 14x + 49, and 49 must be rooted to 7."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Which of these is a perfect square?","choices":["x² + 8x + 8","x² + 16","x² + 8x + 16"],"answer":2,"hint":"The last term must be a square, and the middle term must be twice its root times x.","explain":"x² + 8x + 16 = (x + 4)²: 16 = 4² and 8 = 2 × 4. x² + 16 has no middle term, and 8 is not a square."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Factorise 9x² − 4.","choices":["(9x + 2)(9x − 2)","(3x + 2)(3x − 2)","(3x − 2)²"],"answer":1,"hint":"9x² is the square of 3x, and 4 is the square of 2.","explain":"9x² − 4 = (3x)² − 2² = (3x + 2)(3x − 2). (9x + 2)(9x − 2) forgets to root the 9."}],"explain":{"kid":["Take a square that is x long on each side. Make each side 3 longer. The new square is (x + 3) by (x + 3).","Look at the pieces. There is the old x by x square. There are two thin strips, each x by 3. And there is a small 3 by 3 square in the corner. So (x + 3)² = x² + 3x + 3x + 9, which is x² + 6x + 9.","Now go the other way. Start with x² and cut a 3 by 3 square off one corner. What is left is x² − 9. It looks like an L.","Cut the thin part off the L and turn it. It fits on the end. Now you have a box shape that is x + 3 long and x − 3 wide. So x² − 9 = (x + 3)(x − 3)."],"adult":["Squaring a bracket gives a perfect square: (a + b)² = a² + 2ab + b² and (a − b)² = a² − 2ab + b². The picture shows why the middle term is doubled. A square of side a + b holds one a by a square, two a by b strips and one b by b corner.","To spot one, check that the first and last terms are squares. Then check that the middle term is twice the product of their roots. x² + 10x + 25 = (x + 5)², since 25 = 5² and 10 = 2 × 5. x² + 10x + 20 is not one, because 20 is not a square.","The difference of two squares is a² − b² = (a + b)(a − b). Cut a b by b corner from an a by a square. The L that is left turns into an (a + b) by (a − b) rectangle. Expanding gives a² − ab + ab − b², and the middle terms cancel. It speeds up sums too: 23 × 17 = 20² − 3² = 391, and 21² = 400 + 40 + 1 = 441.","Common mistakes: writing (x + 3)² as x² + 9; keeping only one strip, so x² + 3x + 9; writing (x − 3)² for x² − 9; leaving the number unrooted, as in (x + 9)(x − 9); not rooting the 4 in 4x² − 25, which gives (4x + 5)(4x − 5) instead of (2x + 5)(2x − 5); and trying to factorise a sum such as x² + 9, which has no real factors."]},"familyTip":"Cut a 10 by 10 square from squared paper. Cut a 3 by 3 square from one corner, then snip off the thin strip and move it to the end. Count the new rectangle: 13 by 7 = 91, which is 100 − 9.","keyIdea":"(a + b)² = a² + 2ab + b², so the middle term is doubled. a² − b² = (a + b)(a − b). Root both parts first: 4x² − 25 = (2x + 5)(2x − 5).","worked":{"title":"Factorise 25x² − 36, then check by expanding.","steps":["Both terms are squares: 25x² = (5x)² and 36 = 6². They are subtracted, so this is a difference of two squares.","Use a² − b² = (a + b)(a − b) with a = 5x and b = 6: 25x² − 36 = (5x + 6)(5x − 6).","Check: (5x + 6)(5x − 6) = 25x² − 30x + 30x − 36 = 25x² − 36. The middle terms cancel.","Careful: (25x + 36)(25x − 36) forgets the roots, and (5x − 6)² would expand to 25x² − 60x + 36."]},"visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which kind is each one? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Perfect square","Difference of squares","Neither"],"items":[["x² + 6x + 9",0],["x² − 10x + 25",0],["x² − 16",1],["4x² − 9",1],["x² + 9",2],["x² + 6x + 6",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagsqr","caption":"Flagship lab: grow a square to see (x + b)², then cut a corner off and swing the strip to turn x² − b² into (x + b)(x − b).","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_hTbTvblMUQ":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"If x = 4, what is 3x − 2?","answer":10,"hint":"3x means 3 × 4.","explain":"3 × 4 − 2 = 10."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Can x stand for different numbers in different problems?","choices":["Yes, a letter can stand for any number","No, x is always 10","No, x always means multiply"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about what a letter in algebra represents.","explain":"x can be any number. Within a single problem, the same letter keeps the same value."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which is equal to a + a + a + a?","choices":["4a","a + 4","a4 (a number with digits a and 4)"],"answer":0,"hint":"How many a's are added?","explain":"Four lots of a is 4 × a, written 4a."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"If n = {n}, what is {k}n?","answer":"k*n","hint":"{k}n means {k} × n.","explain":"{k} × {n} = {=k*n}.","params":{"n":[2,9],"k":[2,6]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"If a = {a}, what is {b}a + {c}?","answer":"b*a+c","hint":"Work out {b}a first.","explain":"{b} × {a} + {c} = {=b*a+c}.","params":{"a":[1,9],"b":[2,5],"c":[1,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"If x = {x}, what is x + x + x?","answer":"3*x","hint":"Replace each x with {x}.","explain":"{x} + {x} + {x} = {=3*x} (that's 3x).","params":{"x":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"What does ab mean?","choices":["a × b","a + b","A number with the digits a and b"],"answer":0,"hint":"Letters written next to each other are multiplied.","explain":"ab means a × b."}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"If n = {n}, what is 2n?","answer":"2*n","hint":"2n means 2 × n.","explain":"2 × {n} = {=2*n}.","params":{"n":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"What does 4b mean?","choices":["4 × b","4 + b","A number in the forties"],"answer":0,"hint":"A number written next to a letter means multiply.","explain":"4b means 4 × b."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"If a = 3, what is a + a + a?","answer":9,"hint":"Replace each a with 3.","explain":"3 + 3 + 3 = 9 (and that's 3a)."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"If a = 1, what is 3a + 2?","answer":5,"hint":"Work out 3a first.","explain":"3 × 1 + 2 = 5."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p5","prompt":"If a = {a}, what is a + a + a?","answer":"3*a","hint":"Replace each a with {a}.","explain":"{a} + {a} + {a} = {=3*a} (and that's 3a).","params":{"a":[2,9]}}],"explain":{"kid":["In algebra a letter stands for a number. In 3a, a is a number and 3a means 3 × a. If a = 2, then 3a = 6.","The letter a doesn't mean 'apples'. It's a number we might not know yet, and in some problems it can change."],"adult":["A letter represents a number: sometimes one particular unknown, sometimes any value. The conventions are: 3a means 3 × a, ab means a × b, a/2 means a ÷ 2, and the same letter means the same number throughout one expression or equation.","A common misconception is treating the letter as an object ('3a is three apples'). That breaks down quickly: what would a × a mean?"]},"familyTip":"Make up 'think of a number' puzzles: 'I think of n, double it and add 1, and get 11.' What is n?","keyIdea":"In algebra a letter stands for a number, and 3a means 3 × a.","visual":{"type":"balance","caption":"The bag x hides a number. Keep the scale balanced to find it.","opts":{"mode":"solve","a":1,"b":5,"c":12}}},"mt_i9rJbuFO3p":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"'A number plus {a} is {b}.' Which equation matches?","choices":["n + {a} = {b}","{a}n = {b}","n = {a} + {b}"],"answer":0,"hint":"'Plus' means +.","explain":"n + {a} = {b}.","params":{"a":[2,9],"b":[10,30]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"{a} boxes with the same number of pens hold {=a*k} pens in total. Let p be the pens in one box. Which equation matches?","choices":["{a}p = {=a*k}","p + {a} = {=a*k}","p = {a} × {=a*k}"],"answer":0,"hint":"{a} boxes of p pens is {a} × p.","explain":"{a}p = {=a*k}, so p = {k}.","params":{"a":[2,6],"k":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"'I think of a number and subtract {a}. The answer is {b}.' Which equation matches?","choices":["n − {a} = {b}","{a} − n = {b}","n + {b} = {a}"],"answer":0,"hint":"Start with the number, then subtract {a}.","explain":"n − {a} = {b}.","params":{"a":[2,9],"b":[2,12]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"I think of a number, subtract {a} and get {b}. What was the number?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Undo the subtraction.","explain":"n − {a} = {b}, so n = {b} + {a} = {=a+b}.","params":{"a":[2,9],"b":[2,12]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"'Twice a number is 18.' Which equation matches?","choices":["2n = 18","n + 2 = 18","n² = 18"],"answer":0,"hint":"'Twice' means 2 ×.","explain":"2n = 18, so n = 9."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Before you write an equation for a word problem, what should you decide first?","choices":["What the letter stands for","What the answer is","Which operation is hardest"],"answer":0,"hint":"The letter needs a meaning.","explain":"Say what your letter stands for (for example, m = marbles in one bag) before writing the equation."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"'A number times 4 is 28.' What is the number?","answer":7,"hint":"4n = 28.","explain":"4n = 28, so n = 28 ÷ 4 = 7."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"'A number plus 7 is 12.' Which equation matches?","choices":["n + 7 = 12","7n = 12","n = 7 + 12"],"answer":0,"hint":"'Plus' means +, and 'is' means =.","explain":"n + 7 = 12."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"3 bags with the same number of marbles hold 24 marbles in total. Let m be the number in one bag. Which equation matches?","choices":["3m = 24","m + 3 = 24","m = 3 × 24"],"answer":0,"hint":"3 bags of m marbles is 3 × m.","explain":"3m = 24 (so m = 8)."},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"You had some money, spent £5, and have £8 left. Let x be what you started with. Which equation matches?","choices":["x − 5 = 8","5 − x = 8","x + 8 = 5"],"answer":0,"hint":"Start with x, then take away what you spent.","explain":"x − 5 = 8 (so you started with £13)."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"In n + {a} = {b}, what is n?","answer":"b-a","hint":"What do you add to {a} to make {b}?","explain":"n = {b} − {a} = {=b-a}.","params":{"a":[2,9],"b":[10,20]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"{a} bags with the same number of marbles hold {=a*k} marbles in total. Let m be the number in one bag. Which equation matches?","choices":["{a}m = {=a*k}","m + {a} = {=a*k}","m = {a} × {=a*k}"],"answer":0,"hint":"{a} bags of m marbles is {a} × m.","explain":"{a}m = {=a*k}, so m = {k}.","params":{"a":[2,6],"k":[2,9]}}],"explain":{"kid":["A missing-number puzzle like □ + 7 = 12 can use a letter instead of a box: n + 7 = 12. The letter stands for the number you don't know yet.","To turn a word problem into an equation, pick a letter for the unknown, then write the facts with +, −, × or ÷ and an = sign."],"adult":["Writing an equation is translation: you name the unknown with a letter, then state the relationship the problem describes. 'I think of a number, add 7 and get 12' becomes n + 7 = 12.","Say what your letter means before you start (let m = marbles in one bag). Then check your equation by reading it back in words and seeing whether it matches the story."]},"familyTip":"Make up a 'think of a number' puzzle for someone else, write it as an equation, and see if they can solve it.","keyIdea":"Use a letter for the unknown number, then write what you know as an equation.","visual":{"type":"balance","caption":"2x + 3 = 11 on a scale. Take the same from both sides, then share.","opts":{"mode":"solve","a":2,"b":3,"c":11}}},"mt_Jvg8X0N5u0":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"Perimeter = 2 × (length + width). Length {l} cm, width {w} cm. What is the perimeter in cm?","answer":"2*(l+w)","hint":"Brackets first.","explain":"2 × ({l} + {w}) = 2 × {=l+w} = {=2*(l+w)} cm.","params":{"l":[3,12],"w":[2,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"Cost = price × quantity. Tickets cost £{p} each and you buy {q}. What is the total cost in £?","answer":"p*q","hint":"Multiply the price by the quantity.","explain":"£{p} × {q} = £{=p*q}.","params":{"p":[2,9],"q":[2,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"Distance = speed × time. A cyclist rides at {s} km per hour for {t} hours. How far do they go, in km?","answer":"s*t","hint":"Multiply speed by time.","explain":"{s} × {t} = {=s*t} km.","params":{"s":[8,20],"t":[2,5]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"Number of legs = 4 × number of dogs. How many legs do {n} dogs have?","answer":"4*n","hint":"Put {n} in place of 'number of dogs'.","explain":"4 × {n} = {=4*n} legs.","params":{"n":[2,12]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"Area = length × width. A rug is 3 m by 2 m. What is its area in m²?","answer":6,"hint":"Multiply the length by the width.","explain":"3 × 2 = 6 m²."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Cost = price × quantity. The price is 50p and the quantity is 4. What is the cost?","choices":["£2","£200","54p"],"answer":0,"hint":"50p × 4 = 200p. How many pounds is that?","explain":"50p × 4 = 200p = £2."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"Time = distance ÷ speed. You drive 90 miles at 30 miles per hour. How many hours does it take?","answer":3,"hint":"Divide the distance by the speed.","explain":"90 ÷ 30 = 3 hours."}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"Perimeter = 2 × (length + width). What is the perimeter of a {l} cm by {w} cm rectangle, in cm?","answer":"2*(l+w)","hint":"Add length and width first.","explain":"2 × ({l} + {w}) = 2 × {=l+w} = {=2*(l+w)} cm.","params":{"l":[3,10],"w":[2,8]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Cost = price × quantity. Pens cost £2 each and you buy 7. What is the cost in £?","answer":14,"hint":"Multiply the price by how many.","explain":"£2 × 7 = £14."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"Time = distance ÷ speed. You travel 120 km at 60 km per hour. How many hours does it take?","answer":2,"hint":"Divide the distance by the speed.","explain":"120 ÷ 60 = 2 hours."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"In perimeter = 2 × (length + width), what do you work out first?","choices":["length + width","2 × length","2 × width + length"],"answer":0,"hint":"Brackets first.","explain":"Work out length + width, then double it."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p5","prompt":"Cost = price × quantity. Apples cost {p}p each and you buy {q}. What is the cost in pence?","answer":"p*q","hint":"Multiply the price by how many.","explain":"{p}p × {q} = {=p*q}p.","params":{"p":[10,40],"q":[2,6]}}],"explain":{"kid":["A formula is a rule that tells you how to work something out. For a rectangle: perimeter = 2 × (length + width).","For a rectangle 5 cm long and 3 cm wide: 2 × (5 + 3) = 2 × 8 = 16 cm. Remember: brackets first!"],"adult":["Formulae package up a calculation you would otherwise redo every time: cost = price × quantity, perimeter = 2 × (length + width), time = distance ÷ speed. To use one, replace each word with its value, then calculate in the correct order.","Keep track of units. If the length is in cm, the perimeter is in cm. A cost in pence won't match a price in pounds unless you convert."]},"familyTip":"Measure a table or a book and use perimeter = 2 × (length + width) to find its perimeter. Then check with a tape measure.","keyIdea":"A formula is a rule for working something out: put the numbers in and follow the order of operations.","visual":{"type":"formula","caption":"Change the length and width. The formula P = 2 × (l + w) gives the rectangle's perimeter, worked out step by step.","opts":{"name":"P","expr":"2 × (l + w)","vars":{"l":[1,10,4],"w":[1,6,3]},"pic":"rect","what":"perimeter","unit":"cm"}}},"mt_KwdjWEmMNo":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"If a = {a}, what is a²?","answer":"a*a","hint":"a² = a × a.","explain":"a² = {a} × {a} = {=a*a}.","params":{"a":[2,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"If a = {a} and b = {b}, what is ab?","answer":"a*b","hint":"ab = a × b.","explain":"{a} × {b} = {=a*b}.","params":{"a":[2,9],"b":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which is the same as {k}y?","choices":["y added together {k} times","y to the power {k}","{k} + y"],"answer":0,"hint":"{k}y = {k} × y.","explain":"{k}y = {k} × y, which is y added together {k} times.","params":{"k":[2,6]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"If x = {=2*h}, what is x/2?","answer":"h","hint":"x/2 means x ÷ 2.","explain":"{=2*h} ÷ 2 = {h}.","params":{"h":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which is written the usual way in algebra?","choices":["3ab","ab3","a3b"],"answer":0,"hint":"Numbers go first.","explain":"By convention the number goes first: 3ab."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"What does 2(x + 1) mean?","choices":["2 × (x + 1)","2x + 1","2 + x + 1"],"answer":0,"hint":"A number in front of a bracket multiplies everything in it.","explain":"2(x + 1) = 2 × (x + 1) = 2x + 2."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Is a² the same as 2a?","choices":["No: a² is a × a, but 2a is 2 × a","Yes, always","Only when a is even"],"answer":0,"hint":"Try a = 5.","explain":"With a = 5: a² = 25 but 2a = 10. They're different (they only match when a is 0 or 2)."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"What does 5y mean?","choices":["5 × y","5 + y","y to the power 5"],"answer":0,"hint":"A number next to a letter means multiply.","explain":"5y = 5 × y."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Which is the same as m × m?","choices":["m²","2m","m + m"],"answer":0,"hint":"A letter multiplied by itself is 'squared'.","explain":"m × m = m²."},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"How do you write 'p divided by 4'?","choices":["p/4","4p","4/p"],"answer":0,"hint":"Division is written as a fraction, with p on top.","explain":"p ÷ 4 = p/4."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Work out a² when a = {a}.","answer":"a*a","hint":"a² = a × a.","explain":"{a} × {a} = {=a*a}.","params":{"a":[2,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p5","prompt":"If p = {=4*h}, what is p/4?","answer":"h","hint":"p/4 means p ÷ 4.","explain":"{=4*h} ÷ 4 = {h}.","params":{"h":[2,9]}}],"explain":{"kid":["In algebra we write things the short way. 3y means 3 × y. ab means a × b. a/2 means a ÷ 2.","a² (say 'a squared') means a × a, not 2 × a. So if a = 5, then a² = 25 but 2a = 10."],"adult":["Algebraic notation is shorthand you need to read fluently: 3y = 3 × y = y + y + y; ab = a × b; a² = a × a; a/b = a ÷ b; x/2 is half of x; and brackets group, so 3(a + b) means 3 × (a + b).","Numbers go in front of letters (3y, not y3), and 1y is just written y. Watch for the classic trap: a² is a × a, not 2a."]},"familyTip":"Write your age as an expression in a sentence, such as 'In y years I'll be 13 + y', and get someone to read it back to you.","keyIdea":"Algebra drops the × sign: 3y means 3 × y, ab means a × b, a² means a × a, and a/b means a ÷ b.","visual":{"type":"array","caption":"3n means 3 lots of n. Change n and watch 3n change.","opts":{"rows":3,"cols":4,"label":"n"}}},"mt_vHzVa3SURC":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"v = u + at. If u = {u}, a = {a} and t = {t}, what is v?","answer":"u+a*t","hint":"Multiply a × t first.","explain":"{a} × {t} = {=a*t}, then {u} + {=a*t} = {=u+a*t}.","params":{"u":[1,9],"a":[2,5],"t":[2,6]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"P = 2l + 2w. If l = {l} and w = {w}, what is P?","answer":"2*l+2*w","hint":"2l means 2 × l.","explain":"2 × {l} + 2 × {w} = {=2*l} + {=2*w} = {=2*l+2*w}.","params":{"l":[2,9],"w":[2,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"A = ½bh. If b = {b} and h = {h}, what is A?","answer":"b*h/2","hint":"Multiply b and h, then halve.","explain":"{b} × {h} = {=b*h}, and half of that is {=b*h/2}.","params":{"b":[2,10],"h":[2,10]},"where":"(b*h)%2==0"},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"If x = {x}, what is {k}x²?","answer":"k*x*x","hint":"Square first, then multiply.","explain":"{x}² = {=x*x}, then {k} × {=x*x} = {=k*x*x}.","params":{"x":[2,6],"k":[2,4]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"F = 9C/5 + 32. If C = {=5*k}, what is F?","answer":"9*k+32","hint":"Work out 9C/5 first.","explain":"9 × {=5*k} ÷ 5 = {=9*k}, then {=9*k} + 32 = {=9*k+32}.","params":{"k":[0,8]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"s = d/t. If d = 100 and t = 4, what is s?","answer":25,"hint":"d/t means d ÷ t.","explain":"100 ÷ 4 = 25."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"If a = −3, what is a²?","choices":["9","−9","−6"],"answer":0,"hint":"(−3) × (−3): a negative times a negative is positive.","explain":"(−3)² = (−3) × (−3) = 9."}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"v = u + at. Find v when u = {u}, a = {a} and t = {t}.","answer":"u+a*t","hint":"Multiply a × t first.","explain":"{a} × {t} = {=a*t}, then {u} + {=a*t} = {=u+a*t}.","params":{"u":[1,9],"a":[2,5],"t":[2,6]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"P = 2l + 2w. If l = 5 and w = 3, what is P?","answer":16,"hint":"2l means 2 × l.","explain":"2 × 5 + 2 × 3 = 10 + 6 = 16."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A = ½bh. If b = 6 and h = 4, what is A?","answer":12,"hint":"Multiply b and h, then halve.","explain":"6 × 4 = 24, and half of 24 is 12."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"If x = 4, what is 3x²?","choices":["48","144","24"],"answer":0,"hint":"Square x first, then multiply by 3.","explain":"x² = 16, and 3 × 16 = 48. (144 would be (3x)².)"},{"type":"numeric","id":"p5","prompt":"P = 2l + 2w. Find P when l = {l} and w = {w}.","answer":"2*l+2*w","hint":"2l means 2 × l.","explain":"2 × {l} + 2 × {w} = {=2*l} + {=2*w} = {=2*l+2*w}.","params":{"l":[2,9],"w":[2,9]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Substituting means swapping each letter for its number. If v = u + at, with u = 2, a = 3 and t = 4, then v = 2 + 3 × 4 = 14.","Put the numbers in, then use the order of operations: multiply before you add."],"adult":["Substitution is how you use formulae in science and maths: replace each letter with its value, then evaluate using the order of operations. For v = u + at with u = 2, a = 3 and t = 4: v = 2 + 3 × 4 = 14.","Writing out the substituted line in full, with brackets around negative values, prevents most errors. With a = −2, 3a² means 3 × (−2)² = 3 × 4 = 12."]},"familyTip":"Use F = 9C/5 + 32 to convert today's temperature from °C to °F, and compare it with a weather app.","keyIdea":"Replace each letter with its value, then calculate in the right order.","visual":{"type":"formula","caption":"Slide n. Each time, n is replaced by its value in 3n − 5, then worked out in the right order.","opts":{"name":"y","expr":"3 × n − 5","vars":{"n":[2,10,4]}}}},"mt_EO7DufMP-N":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What is the remainder when x³ + 2x − 5 is divided by (x − 1)?","choices":["−2","−8","−5"],"answer":0,"hint":"Substitute x = 1 into the whole polynomial.","explain":"p(1) = 1 + 2 − 5 = −2. Using p(−1) gives −8, and −5 is only the constant term."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"A quartic is divided by (x − 3). What is the degree of the quotient?","choices":["3","4","2"],"answer":0,"hint":"Dividing by a linear factor lowers the degree by one.","explain":"x⁴ ÷ x = x³, so the quotient is a cubic, degree 3."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"Find the remainder when 2x³ − x + {c} is divided by (x + 1).","answer":"c-1","hint":"(x + 1) means substitute x = −1.","explain":"p(−1) = −2 + 1 + {c} = {=c-1}.","params":{"c":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"p(4) = 7. Which statement is true?","choices":["Dividing p(x) by (x − 4) leaves remainder 7","(x − 4) is a factor of p(x)","(x − 7) is a factor of p(x)"],"answer":0,"hint":"A non-zero value means a non-zero remainder.","explain":"p(4) is the remainder on dividing by (x − 4). It is 7, not 0, so (x − 4) is not a factor."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Given x³ − 4x² + x + 6 = (x + 1)(x² + bx + c), find b and c.","choices":["b = −5, c = 6","b = −4, c = 6","b = −3, c = 6"],"answer":0,"hint":"Match the x² coefficient: 1 + b = −4.","explain":"Expanding gives x³ + (1 + b)x² + (b + c)x + c. So c = 6 and 1 + b = −4, giving b = −5 (check: b + c = 1)."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"2x³ + kx² − 5x + {=4*t-6} has a factor (x − 2). Find k.","answer":"-t","hint":"Set f(2) = 0 and solve for k.","explain":"f(2) = 16 + 4k − 10 + {=4*t-6} = 0, so 4k = −{=4*t} and k = −{t}.","params":{"t":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Which values of a are worth testing as roots of x³ − 7x + 6?","choices":["±1, ±2, ±3, ±6","Only 1, 2, 3 and 6","Any number between −7 and 7"],"answer":0,"hint":"An integer root must divide the constant term.","explain":"For a monic cubic with integer coefficients, an integer root divides 6, so test ±1, ±2, ±3, ±6 (both signs)."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Why does the remainder of p(x) ÷ (x − a) equal p(a)?","choices":["p(x) = (x − a)q(x) + r, and x = a makes the first term 0","Because the remainder is always the constant term","Because a is always a root of p"],"answer":0,"hint":"Write the division as an identity and substitute.","explain":"At x = a the term (x − a)q(x) is zero, leaving p(a) = r."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"x³ + x² − 4x − 4 has a factor (x + 1). Fully factorise it.","choices":["(x + 1)(x − 2)(x + 2)","(x + 1)(x − 4)(x + 1)","(x − 1)(x − 2)(x + 2)"],"answer":0,"hint":"Divide by (x + 1) to get a quadratic, then factorise it.","explain":"x³ + x² − 4x − 4 = (x + 1)(x² − 4) = (x + 1)(x − 2)(x + 2)."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"What is the quotient when x³ − 4x² + x + 6 is divided by (x − 2)?","choices":["x² − 2x − 3","x² − 6x + 13","x² + 2x − 3"],"answer":0,"hint":"Divide the leading term by x, multiply back and subtract, three times.","explain":"x² × (x − 2) = x³ − 2x² leaves −2x² + x + 6; −2x × (x − 2) leaves −3x + 6; −3 × (x − 2) leaves 0. So the quotient is x² − 2x − 3."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Find the remainder when x² + {b}x + 1 is divided by (x − {a}).","answer":"a*a+b*a+1","hint":"The remainder theorem says the remainder is p(a). Substitute x = {a}.","explain":"By the remainder theorem the remainder is p({a}) = {a}² + {b}×{a} + 1 = {=a*a+b*a+1}.","params":{"a":[1,4],"b":[1,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which shows that (x + 2) is a factor of p(x)?","choices":["p(−2) = 0","p(2) = 0","The constant term of p is 2"],"answer":0,"hint":"(x + 2) = (x − a) with a = −2.","explain":"(x + 2) is x − (−2), so the factor theorem needs p(−2) = 0."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"p(x) = x³ + kx − {c}. Given (x − 1) is a factor, find k.","answer":"c-1","hint":"Put x = 1 and set p(1) = 0.","explain":"p(1) = 1 + k − {c} = 0, so k = {c} − 1 = {=c-1}.","params":{"c":[3,12]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Fully factorise x³ − 2x² − 5x + 6.","choices":["(x − 1)(x + 2)(x − 3)","(x + 1)(x − 2)(x + 3)","(x − 1)(x − 2)(x − 3)"],"answer":0,"hint":"Test ±1, ±2, ±3, ±6. Which give 0?","explain":"q(1) = 0, q(−2) = 0 and q(3) = 0, so the factors are (x − 1), (x + 2) and (x − 3)."}],"explain":{"kid":["You can divide one polynomial by another, just like numbers. Divide x³ − 4x² + x + 6 by (x − 2) one term at a time. What times x gives x³? It is x². Multiply back to get x³ − 2x², subtract, and repeat with what is left. The quotient is x² − 2x − 3 and the remainder is 0.","Here is the shortcut. Any division can be written as p(x) = (x − a)q(x) + r. Now put x = a. The bracket becomes 0, so p(a) = r. The remainder when you divide by (x − a) is just p(a). You never have to do the division to find it.","So (x − a) is a factor exactly when p(a) = 0. Which values are worth trying? If the cubic starts with x³ and has whole number coefficients, a must divide the constant term. For x³ − 2x² − 5x + 6, try ±1, ±2, ±3 and ±6. The graph crosses the x-axis at each root you find.","Watch the sign. Dividing by (x + 3) means a = −3, so you work out p(−3), not p(3). Once you have one factor, find the rest by inspection: write p(x) = (x + 1)(ax² + bx + c) and match the coefficients. If a coefficient is unknown, set p(a) equal to the remainder you want and solve."],"adult":["This A-level topic (ages 16 to 18, Common Core A-APR.2 and A-APR.6) is about dividing a polynomial by a linear factor, by long division or by inspection. It also covers the remainder and factor theorems. Learners should divide a cubic by (x − 2), use p(a) to test a factor, and fully factorise a cubic when given one factor.","The key fact is p(x) = (x − a)q(x) + r. Here q has degree one less than p, and r is a number. Put x = a and you get r = p(a). To find roots, use this rule: if a cubic starts with x³ and has whole number coefficients, any whole number root divides the constant term. On a graph, p(a) is the height of the curve at x = a, so zero remainders sit where the curve meets the x-axis.","Common mistakes: using p(−a) instead of p(a), mostly with brackets like (x + 3); taking the constant term as the remainder; sign slips when subtracting in long division; a quotient of the wrong degree; thinking a remainder of 5 means (x − 5) is a factor; and stopping after one factor instead of also factorising the quadratic.","In the lab learners build the quotient of x³ − 4x² + x + 6 ÷ (x − 2) term by term, drag a along the graph to see the remainder equal p(a), test candidate factors of x³ − 2x² − 5x + 6, adjust a, b and c to divide by inspection, and slide k until a factor or remainder condition holds."]},"familyTip":"Ask your teenager to explain, without dividing, why the remainder of p(x) ÷ (x − a) is p(a). If they can write p(x) = (x − a)q(x) + r and substitute x = a, they understand the theorem, not just the trick.","keyIdea":"Dividing p(x) by (x − a) gives p(x) = (x − a)q(x) + r, where the quotient q(x) is one degree lower and the remainder r is a number. Put x = a and the first term vanishes, so r = p(a): this is the remainder theorem. In particular (x − a) is a factor exactly when p(a) = 0 (the factor theorem), which lets you test factors, fully factorise cubics and find unknown coefficients.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"p(x) = x³ − 4x² + x + 6. Work out p(a) for each bracket: is it a factor? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Factor","Not a factor"],"items":[["(x + 1)",0],["(x − 2)",0],["(x − 3)",0],["(x − 1)",1],["(x + 2)",1],["(x − 6)",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagremth","caption":"Flagship lab: long division term by term, drag a to see remainder = p(a), hunt factors, divide by inspection and slide k to meet a factor condition.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_FX4a2Q8XXN":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Expand {a}(x + {b}).","choices":["{a}x + {=a*b}","{a}x + {b}","x + {=a*b}"],"answer":0,"hint":"Multiply both terms by {a}.","explain":"{a} × x + {a} × {b} = {a}x + {=a*b}.","params":{"a":[2,9],"b":[1,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"{a}(x + {b}) = {a}x + ?","answer":"a*b","hint":"Multiply {b} by {a}.","explain":"{a} × {b} = {=a*b}.","params":{"a":[2,9],"b":[1,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Expand {a}({b}x − {c}).","choices":["{=a*b}x − {=a*c}","{=a*b}x − {c}","{=a+b}x − {=a*c}"],"answer":0,"hint":"Multiply both terms by {a}.","explain":"{a} × {b}x = {=a*b}x and {a} × {c} = {=a*c}, so you get {=a*b}x − {=a*c}.","params":{"a":[2,6],"b":[2,6],"c":[1,9]},"where":"a*b!=a+b"},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"Check by substituting: if x = {x}, what is {a}(x + {b})?","answer":"a*(x+b)","hint":"Work out the bracket first.","explain":"{a} × ({x} + {b}) = {a} × {=x+b} = {=a*(x+b)}.","params":{"a":[2,9],"b":[1,9],"x":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Expand 5(2a + 3b).","choices":["10a + 15b","10a + 3b","7a + 8b"],"answer":0,"hint":"Multiply both terms by 5.","explain":"5 × 2a = 10a and 5 × 3b = 15b."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which expansion is correct?","choices":["6(x − 1) = 6x − 6","6(x − 1) = 6x − 1","6(x − 1) = 6x + 6"],"answer":0,"hint":"Multiply both terms by 6.","explain":"6 × x − 6 × 1 = 6x − 6."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Expand −3(y + 2).","choices":["−3y − 6","−3y + 6","−3y + 2"],"answer":0,"hint":"−3 × +2 is negative.","explain":"−3 × y = −3y and −3 × 2 = −6."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Expand 3(x + 4).","choices":["3x + 12","3x + 4","x + 12"],"answer":0,"hint":"Multiply both x and 4 by 3.","explain":"3 × x + 3 × 4 = 3x + 12."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Expand 2(5 − y).","choices":["10 − 2y","10 − y","7 − 2y"],"answer":0,"hint":"Multiply both 5 and y by 2.","explain":"2 × 5 − 2 × y = 10 − 2y."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"{a}(x + {b}) = {a}x + □. What number goes in the box?","answer":"a*b","hint":"Multiply {b} by {a}.","explain":"{a} × {b} = {=a*b}, so {a}(x + {b}) = {a}x + {=a*b}.","params":{"a":[2,6],"b":[1,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Expand −2(x − 3).","choices":["−2x + 6","−2x − 6","−2x − 3"],"answer":0,"hint":"−2 × −3 is positive.","explain":"−2 × x = −2x and −2 × −3 = +6, so you get −2x + 6."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Expand {a}(x − {b}).","choices":["{a}x − {=a*b}","{a}x − {b}","{a}x + {=a*b}"],"answer":0,"hint":"Multiply both terms by {a}, and keep the minus sign.","explain":"{a} × x − {a} × {b} = {a}x − {=a*b}.","params":{"a":[2,6],"b":[1,6]}}],"explain":{"kid":["To expand a bracket, multiply each thing inside by the number outside. 3(x + 4) = 3 × x + 3 × 4 = 3x + 12.","Think of 3 bags, each holding x sweets plus 4 more sweets: altogether you have 3x + 12 sweets."],"adult":["Expanding uses the distributive law: a(b + c) = ab + ac. So 3(2x + 5) = 6x + 15, and 4(x − 2) = 4x − 8.","The most common slip is multiplying only the first term: 3(x + 4) is not 3x + 4. With a negative outside, every sign inside flips: −2(x − 3) = −2x + 6."]},"familyTip":"Check an expansion by substituting a number: if x = 2, then 3(x + 4) = 18 and 3x + 12 = 18. If the two disagree, there's a mistake.","keyIdea":"Multiply everything inside the bracket by the term outside: 3(2x + 5) = 6x + 15.","visual":{"type":"array","caption":"Drag the split: 3 × (2 + 4) is 3 × 2 plus 3 × 4.","opts":{"rows":3,"cols":6,"split":2}}},"mt_4t_C2LgbMj":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What is 1/(x − 1) + 2/(x + 1) as one fraction?","choices":["(3x − 1)/((x − 1)(x + 1))","3/(2x)","(3x + 1)/((x − 1)(x + 1))"],"answer":0,"hint":"Use the common denominator (x − 1)(x + 1).","explain":"The top is (x + 1) + 2(x − 1) = 3x − 1, over (x − 1)(x + 1)."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"({=a+b}x + {=a-b})/((x − 1)(x + 1)) = A/(x − 1) + B/(x + 1). Find A.","answer":"a","hint":"Put x = 1 to make the (x − 1) factor zero.","explain":"A(x + 1) + B(x − 1) = {=a+b}x + {=a-b}. Put x = 1: 2A = {=a+b} + {=a-b} = {=2*a}, so A = {a}.","params":{"a":[5,8],"b":[1,4]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"({=a+b}x + {=a-b})/((x − 1)(x + 1)) = A/(x − 1) + B/(x + 1). Find B.","answer":"b","hint":"Put x = −1 so the A term vanishes.","explain":"Put x = −1: the right side is −2B and the top is −{=a+b} + {=a-b} = −{=2*b}. So −2B = −{=2*b} and B = {b}.","params":{"a":[5,8],"b":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Before splitting (x² + 3)/((x − 1)(x + 2)) into partial fractions you should…","choices":["Divide, because the top degree equals the bottom degree","Use cover-up straight away","Factorise x² + 3 first"],"answer":0,"hint":"Compare the degree of the top with the degree of the bottom.","explain":"Top and bottom both have degree 2, so the fraction is improper. Divide first to get 1 plus a proper fraction."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"To find B in (2x + 7)/((x − 1)(x + 3)) = A/(x − 1) + B/(x + 3), which value do you substitute?","choices":["x = −3","x = 3","x = 0"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which x makes (x + 3) equal zero?","explain":"x = −3 makes (x + 3) zero, which removes the A term. Then 2(−3) + 7 = −4B, so B = −1/4."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Why is A/(x − 2) + B/(x − 2) the wrong form for a repeated factor (x − 2)²?","choices":["It is really one term, (A + B)/(x − 2)","It has too many constants","It should be A/(x + 2)"],"answer":0,"hint":"Add the two fractions. What do you get?","explain":"A/(x − 2) + B/(x − 2) = (A + B)/(x − 2), a single term. You need A/(x − 2) + B/(x − 2)²."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"In 4/((x − 1)²(x + 1)) = A/(x − 1) + B/(x − 1)² + C/(x + 1), B = 2 and C = 1. What is A?","choices":["−1","1","2"],"answer":0,"hint":"Compare x² terms: A(x − 1)(x + 1) gives Ax², C(x − 1)² gives Cx².","explain":"There is no x² on the left, so A + C = 0 and A = −1."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"3/(x(x + 3)) = A/x + B/(x + 3). Find B.","answer":-1,"hint":"Cover (x + 3), then put x = −3 into what is left, 3/x.","explain":"Cover (x + 3) and put x = −3: 3 ÷ (−3) = −1, so B = −1."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"Kai writes 6/((x − 1)(x + 2)) = 2/(x − 1) − 2/(x + 2). How can he check it quickly?","choices":["Put x = 0 into both sides","Count the fractions","Differentiate both sides"],"answer":0,"hint":"Any x that is not a root of the bottom will do.","explain":"At x = 0: left 6 ÷ (−2) = −3, right −2 − 1 = −3. They agree, so the split is right."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m10","prompt":"{=3*c}/((x − 2)(x + 1)) = A/(x − 2) + B/(x + 1). Find A.","answer":"c","hint":"Cover (x − 2) and put x = 2.","explain":"Put x = 2: {=3*c} ÷ (2 + 1) = {c}, so A = {c} and B = −{c}.","params":{"c":[1,4]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which is the right form for (3x + 4)/((x + 1)(x − 3))?","choices":["A/(x + 1) + B/(x − 3)","A/(x + 1)(x − 3)","(Ax + B)/(x + 1)"],"answer":0,"hint":"Each linear factor on the bottom gets its own fraction with a constant on top.","explain":"Two different linear factors give two fractions: A/(x + 1) + B/(x − 3)."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"({=a+b}x + {=2*a-b})/((x − 1)(x + 2)) = A/(x − 1) + B/(x + 2). Find A.","answer":"a","hint":"Cover (x − 1) and put x = 1 into everything that is left.","explain":"Put x = 1: ({=a+b} + {=2*a-b}) ÷ (1 + 2) = {=3*a} ÷ 3 = {a}, so A = {a}.","params":{"a":[2,5],"b":[1,3]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"({=a+b}x + {=2*a-b})/((x − 1)(x + 2)) = A/(x − 1) + B/(x + 2). Find B.","answer":"b","hint":"For (x + 2), put x = −2, not x = 2.","explain":"Put x = −2: (−{=2*a+2*b} + {=2*a-b}) ÷ (−2 − 1) = −{=3*b} ÷ −3 = {b}, so B = {b}.","params":{"a":[2,5],"b":[1,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"What form does 5/((x − 2)²(x + 1)) need?","choices":["A/(x − 2) + B/(x − 2)² + C/(x + 1)","A/(x − 2)² + B/(x + 1)","A/(x − 2) + B/(x − 2) + C/(x + 1)"],"answer":0,"hint":"A squared factor needs one fraction for each power.","explain":"The repeated factor (x − 2)² needs A/(x − 2) and B/(x − 2)², plus C/(x + 1) for the other factor."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p5","prompt":"{=k*m}/(x(x + {k})) = A/x + B/(x + {k}). Find A.","answer":"m","hint":"Cover x and put x = 0 into what is left.","explain":"Put x = 0: {=k*m} ÷ (0 + {k}) = {m}. So A = {m} (and B = −{m}).","params":{"k":[2,5],"m":[1,4]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Adding 2/(x + 1) and 3/(x − 2) gives one fraction: (5x − 1)/((x + 1)(x − 2)). Partial fractions goes the other way. You start with the single fraction and split it back into simple parts.","Each factor on the bottom gets its own fraction. To split (x + 5)/((x − 1)(x + 2)), write A/(x − 1) + B/(x + 2). Put both over the same bottom. The tops must then be equal: A(x + 2) + B(x − 1) = x + 5.","Now choose x values that make terms vanish. Put x = 1 and the B part is zero, so 3A = 6 and A = 2. Put x = −2 and the A part is zero, so −3B = 3 and B = −1. This shortcut is called cover-up. Watch the sign: for (x + 2) you use x = −2.","Two special cases. A squared factor like (x − 1)² needs two fractions, A/(x − 1) and B/(x − 1)². And if the top's degree is the same as the bottom's or higher, divide first. Then split what is left over."],"adult":["This A-level topic (ages 16 to 18) is about splitting one fraction in x into a sum of simpler ones. The bottom is made of linear factors, some of them repeated, and each new top is a constant. Learners need it to integrate fractions and to expand them as series, so it opens up a lot of later calculus.","The method rests on one fact. Once both sides share a bottom, the tops must agree for every x. That gives two tools. Put in the root of each factor (cover-up) to get its constant at once. Or compare the x², x and number terms to get the rest. You need this second tool for the lower power of a repeated factor. To check, add the parts back or test one x.","Common mistakes: adding fractions by adding tops and bottoms; using x = a for a factor (x + a); giving a repeated factor (x − a)² only one fraction; writing A/(x − a) + B/(x − a), which is really one term; and splitting an improper fraction without dividing first. Be careful with cover-up on a repeated factor: it only gives the top over the highest power.","In the lab learners add two fractions to see where the split comes from, drag A and B until A/(x − 1) + B/(x + 2) sits on the target curve, and find constants by covering one factor at a time. They also choose the right form for (x − 1)² and solve for A, B and C, and see on a graph why an improper fraction must be divided first."]},"keyIdea":"Partial fractions splits one fraction into a sum of simpler ones, which is adding fractions in reverse. Each linear factor (x − a) in the denominator gets its own A/(x − a). A repeated factor (x − a)² needs both A/(x − a) and B/(x − a)². Find the constants by substituting the x that makes a factor zero (cover-up) or by comparing coefficients. If the top degree is not lower than the bottom, divide first.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which denominators need more than one fraction per factor? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["One term per factor","Has a repeated factor"],"items":[["(x − 1)(x + 2)",0],["x(x + 3)",0],["(x − 2)(x + 2)(x + 1)",0],["(x − 1)²",1],["(x + 2)²(x − 3)",1],["x²(x + 1)",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagpfrac","caption":"Flagship lab: add two fractions, drag A and B to match a curve, use cover-up, handle a repeated factor, and divide an improper fraction first.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_aZENxGikHn":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which expression means '{k} less than n'?","choices":["n − {k}","{k} − n","{k}n"],"answer":0,"hint":"Start with n and take away {k}.","explain":"n − {k}.","params":{"k":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which expression means '{c} more than {k} times a number n'?","choices":["{k}n + {c}","{k}(n + {c})","{c}n + {k}"],"answer":0,"hint":"Build it from the inside: {k} times n first.","explain":"{k}n + {c}.","params":{"k":[2,9],"c":[1,9]},"where":"k!=c"},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"If n = {n}, what is the value of '{c} more than {k} times n'?","answer":"k*n+c","hint":"Write it as {k}n + {c}, then substitute.","explain":"{k} × {n} + {c} = {=k*n+c}.","params":{"k":[2,9],"c":[1,9],"n":[1,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which expression means 'n divided by {k}'?","choices":["n/{k}","{k}/n","n − {k}"],"answer":0,"hint":"n goes on top.","explain":"n ÷ {k} = n/{k}.","params":{"k":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which expression means 'the product of a and b'?","choices":["ab","a + b","a/b"],"answer":0,"hint":"'Product' means multiply.","explain":"ab = a × b."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which expression means 'twice the sum of x and 3'?","choices":["2(x + 3)","2x + 3","x + 3 + 2"],"answer":0,"hint":"Find the sum first, then double it.","explain":"The sum is x + 3; twice that is 2(x + 3)."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Sam is s years old. His sister is 4 years younger. Which expression gives her age?","choices":["s − 4","4 − s","s + 4"],"answer":0,"hint":"Younger means fewer years.","explain":"s − 4."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which expression means '{k} more than n'?","choices":["n + {k}","{k}n","{k} − n"],"answer":0,"hint":"'More than' means add.","explain":"n + {k}.","params":{"k":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Which expression means '3 less than n'?","choices":["n − 3","3 − n","3n"],"answer":0,"hint":"Start with n, then take 3 away.","explain":"n − 3."},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which expression means '5 more than twice a number n'?","choices":["2n + 5","2(n + 5)","5n + 2"],"answer":0,"hint":"Twice n is 2n. Then add 5.","explain":"2n + 5."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Which expression means 'a number n shared equally between 4 people'?","choices":["n/4","4/n","n − 4"],"answer":0,"hint":"Sharing means dividing n.","explain":"n ÷ 4 = n/4."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p5","prompt":"If n = {n}, what is the value of 'twice n, minus {c}'?","answer":"2*n-c","hint":"Write it as 2n − {c}, then substitute.","explain":"2 × {n} − {c} = {=2*n-c}.","params":{"n":[3,9],"c":[1,5]}}],"explain":{"kid":["You can turn words into algebra. If n is a number, '5 more than n' is n + 5, 'twice n' is 2n, and '3 less than n' is n − 3.","Careful with 'less than': '3 less than n' means start with n and take 3 away. That's n − 3, not 3 − n."],"adult":["Turning words into expressions is mostly vocabulary: 'more than' or 'increased by' means +; 'less than' or 'decreased by' means −; 'twice' or 'the product of' means ×; 'shared between' or 'the quotient of' means ÷.","Order matters for − and ÷: '7 less than n' is n − 7, and 'n divided by 3' is n/3. For combined phrases, build from the inside out: '5 more than twice a number' is 2n + 5."]},"familyTip":"Describe a phone plan or bus fare in words (such as '£10 a month plus £2 per extra GB'), then write it as an expression.","keyIdea":"Translate phrase by phrase, and watch the order for subtraction and division.","visual":{"type":"formula","caption":"'Take 12 from a number x, then halve it' becomes (x − 12) ÷ 2. Slide x and watch it work out.","opts":{"name":"answer","expr":"(x − 12) ÷ 2","vars":{"x":[12,60,50]}}}},"mt_QhFEDyIwSO":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"Solve {a}x + {b} = {=a*x+b}.","answer":"x","hint":"Subtract {b}, then divide by {a}.","explain":"{a}x = {=a*x}, so x = {x}. Check: {a} × {x} + {b} = {=a*x+b}.","params":{"a":[2,9],"b":[1,15],"x":[1,10]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"Solve {a}x − {b} = {=a*x-b}.","answer":"x","hint":"Add {b} to both sides first.","explain":"{a}x = {=a*x}, so x = {x}.","params":{"a":[2,9],"b":[1,15],"x":[2,10]},"where":"a*x>b"},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"Solve {a}(x + {b}) = {=a*(x+b)}.","answer":"x","hint":"Divide both sides by {a} first.","explain":"x + {b} = {=x+b}, so x = {x}.","params":{"a":[2,6],"b":[1,6],"x":[1,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"Solve {a}x + {b} = {c}x + {=(a-c)*x+b}.","answer":"x","hint":"Subtract {c}x from both sides first.","explain":"{=a-c}x + {b} = {=(a-c)*x+b}, so {=a-c}x = {=(a-c)*x} and x = {x}.","params":{"a":[5,9],"c":[2,4],"b":[1,9],"x":[1,9]},"where":"a-c>=2"},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"Solve x/{d} + {b} = {=k+b}.","answer":"d*k","hint":"Subtract {b}, then multiply by {d}.","explain":"x/{d} = {k}, so x = {k} × {d} = {=d*k}.","params":{"d":[2,5],"k":[1,6],"b":[1,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which step is correct for 4x − 5 = 23?","choices":["Add 5 to both sides to get 4x = 28","Subtract 5 from both sides to get 4x = 18","Divide only 4x by 4 to get x − 5 = 23"],"answer":0,"hint":"Undo the − 5, and do it to both sides.","explain":"Adding 5 to both sides gives 4x = 28, so x = 7."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"Solve 7 − x = 2.","answer":5,"hint":"What do you take from 7 to leave 2?","explain":"7 − 5 = 2, so x = 5. (Or add x to both sides: 7 = 2 + x.)"}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"Find x: {a}x + {b} = {=a*x+b}.","answer":"x","hint":"Subtract {b} from both sides, then divide by {a}.","explain":"{a}x = {=a*x}, so x = {x}.","params":{"a":[2,6],"b":[1,9],"x":[1,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Solve 5x − 7 = 18.","answer":5,"hint":"Add 7 to both sides first.","explain":"5x = 25, so x = 5."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"Solve 3(x + 1) = 18.","answer":5,"hint":"Expand, or divide both sides by 3 first.","explain":"x + 1 = 6, so x = 5."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Solve 5x + 2 = 2x + 14.","answer":4,"hint":"Subtract 2x from both sides first.","explain":"3x + 2 = 14, then 3x = 12, so x = 4."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p5","prompt":"Find x: {a}(x − {b}) = {=a*(x-b)}.","answer":"x","hint":"Divide both sides by {a} first.","explain":"x − {b} = {=x-b}, so x = {x}.","params":{"a":[2,5],"b":[1,4],"x":[2,9]},"where":"x>b"}],"explain":{"kid":["To solve 2x + 3 = 11, undo the + 3 first (take 3 from both sides: 2x = 8), then undo the × 2 (divide both sides by 2: x = 4).","If there's a bracket, you can expand it first: 3(x + 1) = 18 becomes 3x + 3 = 18."],"adult":["Solving a linear equation means isolating the unknown using operations that keep both sides equal. Undo things in reverse order: for 5x − 7 = 18, add 7 (5x = 25), then divide by 5 (x = 5).","When x appears on both sides, gather the x terms on one side first: 5x + 2 = 2x + 14 → 3x + 2 = 14 → 3x = 12 → x = 4. Always check by substituting back into the original equation."]},"familyTip":"After solving, substitute your answer back into the original equation. If both sides match, you know you're right without looking at the answers.","keyIdea":"Undo operations in reverse order, doing the same to both sides. If x is on both sides, gather the x terms first.","visual":{"type":"balance","caption":"Solve 3x + 4 = 19 by keeping the scale level.","opts":{"mode":"solve","a":3,"b":4,"c":19}},"lab":{"type":"flageq","caption":"Flagship lab: the market scale. Drag cubes and bags off the pans; the beam tips the moment the sides stop matching.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_mmUVPwnv3o":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"A taxi charges £{f} plus £{r} per mile. The fare was £{=f+r*m}. How many miles was the trip?","answer":"m","hint":"Write {f} + {r}m = {=f+r*m}.","explain":"{r}m = {=r*m}, so m = {m}. Check: {f} + {r} × {m} = {=f+r*m} ✓.","params":{"f":[2,5],"r":[2,4],"m":[2,12]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"Mia has some sweets. She gets {a} more and now has {=s+a}. How many did she have at first?","answer":"s","hint":"s + {a} = {=s+a}.","explain":"s = {=s+a} − {a} = {s}.","params":{"a":[3,9],"s":[5,20]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"{n} friends share a bill equally and each pays £{k}. What was the bill in £?","answer":"n*k","hint":"b ÷ {n} = {k}.","explain":"b = {k} × {n} = {=n*k}.","params":{"n":[2,5],"k":[5,15]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"A rectangle's length is {d} cm more than its width. Its perimeter is {=4*w+2*d} cm. What is the width in cm?","answer":"w","hint":"Perimeter = 2(w + w + {d}) = 4w + {=2*d}.","explain":"4w + {=2*d} = {=4*w+2*d}, so 4w = {=4*w} and w = {w}.","params":{"w":[2,10],"d":[1,5]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"Two consecutive whole numbers add up to {=2*x+1}. What is the smaller one?","answer":"x","hint":"Call them n and n + 1.","explain":"n + n + 1 = {=2*x+1}, so 2n = {=2*x} and n = {x}.","params":{"x":[5,20]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"You're solving a problem about the number of people on a bus and get x = −4. What should you do?","choices":["Recheck the equation: a negative number of people doesn't make sense","Write −4 as the answer","Round it to 4"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does the answer fit the story?","explain":"Answers must make sense in context. A negative count means the equation or the working has a mistake."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"Ali is 3 times as old as his sister. Together they are 32. How old is his sister?","answer":8,"hint":"Let her age be s. Then Ali is 3s, and s + 3s = 32.","explain":"4s = 32, so s = 8 (and Ali is 24). Check: 8 + 24 = 32 ✓."}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"Tom has some stickers. He gets {a} more and now has {=s+a}. How many did he have at the start?","answer":"s","hint":"s + {a} = {=s+a}.","explain":"s = {=s+a} − {a} = {s}. Check: {s} + {a} = {=s+a} ✓.","params":{"a":[3,9],"s":[5,20]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A taxi charges £3 plus £2 per mile. The fare was £17. How many miles was the trip?","answer":7,"hint":"3 + 2m = 17.","explain":"2m = 14, so m = 7."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"Three friends share a bill equally and each pays £12. What was the total bill in £?","answer":36,"hint":"b ÷ 3 = 12.","explain":"b = 12 × 3 = 36."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A rectangle's length is 3 cm more than its width. Its perimeter is 26 cm. What is the width in cm?","answer":5,"hint":"Let the width be w. Then the length is w + 3, and the perimeter is 2(w + w + 3).","explain":"2(2w + 3) = 26, so 4w + 6 = 26, 4w = 20 and w = 5. Check: 2 × (5 + 8) = 26 ✓."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p5","prompt":"A gym charges a £{f} joining fee plus £{r} a week. Sam paid £{=f+r*w} in total. How many weeks was that?","answer":"w","hint":"Write {f} + {r}w = {=f+r*w}.","explain":"{r}w = {=r*w}, so w = {w}. Check: {f} + {r} × {w} = {=f+r*w} ✓.","params":{"f":[10,30],"r":[5,9],"w":[2,10]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Word problems get easier once you write an equation. 'Tom has some stickers. He gets 6 more and now has 20.' Let s be Tom's stickers at the start: s + 6 = 20, so s = 14.","Always check: 14 + 6 = 20 ✓. Also ask whether the answer makes sense: you can't have −3 stickers."],"adult":["A reliable routine: (1) say what the letter stands for, (2) write the equation from the story, (3) solve it, (4) check it against the original words, not just your equation.","Example: a taxi charges £3 plus £2 per mile and the fare was £17. Let m = miles: 3 + 2m = 17, so 2m = 14 and m = 7. Check: £3 + 7 × £2 = £17 ✓."]},"familyTip":"Find a real pricing rule (a taxi, a gym, a phone plan) and work out the unknown from a total, such as how many miles a £20 fare covers.","keyIdea":"Name the unknown, write an equation, solve it, then check the answer makes sense.","visual":{"type":"balance","caption":"'4 tickets plus a £5 fee cost £33': 4x + 5 = 33 on a scale.","opts":{"mode":"solve","a":4,"b":5,"c":33}}},"mt_q0LJXMQe70":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"Find the gradient of the line through ({p}, {q}) and ({=p+r}, {=q+m*r}).","answer":"m","hint":"Rise ÷ run.","explain":"Rise {=m*r} ÷ run {r} = {m}.","params":{"p":[0,5],"q":[0,5],"r":[1,4],"m":[1,5]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"Find the gradient of the line through (0, {=q+m*r}) and ({r}, {q}).","answer":"0-m","hint":"y goes down, so the gradient is negative.","explain":"Rise {q} − {=q+m*r} = −{=m*r}, run {r}, so the gradient is −{m}.","params":{"q":[0,5],"r":[1,4],"m":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"What is the gradient of a horizontal line?","choices":["0","1","It has no gradient"],"answer":0,"hint":"How much does y change?","explain":"y doesn't change, so rise = 0 and the gradient is 0."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"A line has gradient {m} and passes through ({x}, {=m*x+c}). Find c.","answer":"c","hint":"Substitute into y = {m}x + c.","explain":"{=m*x+c} = {m} × {x} + c = {=m*x} + c, so c = {c}.","params":{"m":[2,5],"x":[1,4],"c":[1,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which equation is the line with gradient 3 that crosses the y-axis at −4?","choices":["y = 3x − 4","y = −4x + 3","y = 3x + 4"],"answer":0,"hint":"y = mx + c.","explain":"m = 3 and c = −4, so y = 3x − 4."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Ana says the gradient from (2, 1) to (6, 9) is 4 ÷ 8 = 0.5. What went wrong?","choices":["She did run ÷ rise; it should be 8 ÷ 4 = 2","She should add, not divide","Nothing: 0.5 is right"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which goes on top?","explain":"Rise is 9 − 1 = 8 and run is 6 − 2 = 4. Gradient = rise ÷ run = 2."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"A line goes down from left to right. Its gradient is…","choices":["negative","positive","zero"],"answer":0,"hint":"As x increases, y decreases.","explain":"y falls as x rises, so the gradient is negative."}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"Find the gradient of the line through (1, 3) and (3, 7).","answer":2,"hint":"Rise ÷ run.","explain":"Rise 7 − 3 = 4 and run 3 − 1 = 2, so the gradient is 4 ÷ 2 = 2."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Find the gradient of the line from (0, {b}) to ({r}, {=b+m*r}).","answer":"m","hint":"Rise ÷ run.","explain":"Rise {=m*r}, run {r}, so the gradient is {=m*r} ÷ {r} = {m}.","params":{"b":[0,9],"r":[1,5],"m":[1,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"In y = 5x − 2, what is the gradient?","choices":["5","−2","5x"],"answer":0,"hint":"Compare with y = mx + c.","explain":"m is the number in front of x, so the gradient is 5."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A line has gradient {m} and passes through (1, {=m+c}). Find c in y = mx + c.","answer":"c","hint":"Put x = 1 and y = {=m+c} into y = {m}x + c.","explain":"{=m+c} = {m} × 1 + c, so c = {c}.","params":{"m":[2,9],"c":[1,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Which line is steeper: y = {a}x + 1 or y = {b}x + 9?","choices":["y = {a}x + 1","y = {b}x + 9","They are equally steep"],"answer":0,"hint":"Steepness comes from m, not c.","explain":"The gradients are {a} and {b}. The +9 only moves the line up; it doesn't make it steeper.","params":{"a":[2,9],"b":[1,8]},"where":"a>b"}],"explain":{"kid":["Gradient tells you how steep a line is. Pick two points. Gradient = change in y ÷ change in x. From (1, 3) to (3, 7), y goes up 4 while x goes up 2. So the gradient is 4 ÷ 2 = 2.","Every straight line can be written as y = mx + c. Here m is the gradient. c is where the line crosses the y-axis. This line is y = 2x + 1."],"adult":["Gradient = (y₂ − y₁) ÷ (x₂ − x₁), or rise over run. A line going down from left to right has a negative gradient. A flat line has gradient 0.","To find the equation, work out m. Then put one point into y = mx + c to find c. Through (1, 3) with m = 2: 3 = 2 + c, so c = 1 and y = 2x + 1. In the US this is often written y = mx + b."]},"familyTip":"Find a ramp or a step. Measure how far it rises and how far it runs, then work out its gradient.","keyIdea":"A straight line is y = mx + c. The gradient m is the steepness, and c is where the line crosses the y-axis.","visual":{"type":"plot","caption":"Drag the two points. The gradient is rise ÷ run.","opts":{"mode":"line","p":[1,3],"q":[3,7],"xr":[-1,6],"yr":[-2,12]}},"lab":{"type":"flagline","caption":"Flagship lab: the night taxi. Drag the taxi, then drag the intercept and gradient handles until the line fits.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_yYs2sYPwjl":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"What is the leading coefficient of {c} + {b}x + {a}x²?","answer":"a","hint":"Write it in standard form first.","explain":"Standard form is {a}x² + {b}x + {c}, so the leading coefficient is {a}.","params":{"a":[2,9],"b":[2,9],"c":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which is 5 − 3x² + 2x in standard form?","choices":["−3x² + 2x + 5","5 + 2x − 3x²","2x − 3x² + 5"],"answer":0,"hint":"Highest power first.","explain":"x² first, then x, then the number: −3x² + 2x + 5."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"What is the degree of {a}x^{n} − x^{k}?","answer":"n","hint":"The biggest power wins.","explain":"The powers are {n} and {k}, so the degree is {n}.","params":{"a":[2,9],"n":[4,7],"k":[1,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"What is 7 called?","choices":["A monomial of degree 0","A binomial","Not a polynomial"],"answer":0,"hint":"One term, no x.","explain":"A single number is a monomial with degree 0."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which is NOT a polynomial?","choices":["1/x + 2","x² − 1","5x"],"answer":0,"hint":"Powers must be whole and not negative.","explain":"1/x is x⁻¹, a negative power, so it is not a polynomial."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"A degree-2 polynomial is called…","choices":["quadratic","linear","cubic"],"answer":0,"hint":"Degree 1 is linear, 3 is cubic.","explain":"Degree 2 is quadratic."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"How many terms are in x^{n} + x^{k} + x + {c}?","answer":"4","hint":"Count them.","explain":"There are 4 terms.","params":{"n":[5,8],"k":[2,4],"c":[1,9]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"How many terms does 4x³ − x + 7 have?","choices":["3","2","4"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count the parts joined by + or −.","explain":"4x³, −x and 7: three terms, so it is a trinomial."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"What is the degree of {a}x^{n} + {b}x + 1?","answer":"n","hint":"Highest power of x.","explain":"The highest power is {n}, so the degree is {n}.","params":{"a":[2,9],"n":[3,6],"b":[2,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"What is the constant term of {a}x² − 3x + {c}?","answer":"c","hint":"The plain number.","explain":"The constant term is {c}.","params":{"a":[2,9],"c":[2,20]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Which is a binomial?","choices":["x² + 5","3x","x² + x + 1"],"answer":0,"hint":"Bi means two.","explain":"x² + 5 has two terms."}],"explain":{"kid":["A term is a number, a letter, or both multiplied, like 3x². Terms are joined by + or −.","One term is a monomial. Two terms make a binomial. Three make a trinomial. The degree is the biggest power of x."],"adult":["Standard form writes the terms from the highest power down: 5 − 3x² + 2x becomes −3x² + 2x + 5.","In standard form the first number is the leading coefficient (−3), and the plain number is the constant term (5). The degree is 2, so this is a quadratic trinomial."]},"familyTip":"Write a few expressions on cards. Take turns naming each one: how many terms, what degree?","keyIdea":"A polynomial is a sum of terms; its degree is the highest power, and standard form lists terms from highest power to lowest.","visual":{"type":"polyterms","caption":"Tap a term, change it, add or remove terms. The names update as you go.","opts":{"terms":[[5,0],[-3,2],[2,1]]}}},"mt_93GREOFJAe":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"Simplify 2^{a} × 2^{b} as 2 to what power?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Same base, add.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b}.","params":{"a":[2,9],"b":[2,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"Simplify y^{a} ÷ y^{b}. What is the power?","answer":"a-b","hint":"Same base, subtract.","explain":"{a} − {b} = {=a-b}.","params":{"a":[9,15],"b":[2,8]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"Simplify (a^{p})^{q}. What is the power?","answer":"p*q","hint":"Multiply.","explain":"{p} × {q} = {=p*q}.","params":{"p":[2,6],"q":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which can the laws NOT combine into one power?","choices":["2³ × 3²","2³ × 2²","5⁴ ÷ 5"],"answer":0,"hint":"Bases must match.","explain":"2 and 3 are different bases."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"{c}x^{a} × {d}x^{b} = kx^n. What is k?","answer":"c*d","hint":"Multiply the numbers, add the powers.","explain":"{c} × {d} = {=c*d}.","params":{"c":[2,6],"d":[2,6],"a":[2,5],"b":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"x⁷ ÷ x⁷ = ?","choices":["1","0","x"],"answer":0,"hint":"Anything divided by itself.","explain":"Every factor cancels: the answer is 1."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"(x²)³ is…","choices":["x⁶","x⁵","x⁸"],"answer":0,"hint":"Three lots of x².","explain":"x² × x² × x² = x⁶."}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"x^{a} × x^{b} = x^? What is the power?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Add the powers.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b}.","params":{"a":[2,9],"b":[2,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"x^{a} ÷ x^{b} = x^? What is the power?","answer":"a-b","hint":"Subtract the powers.","explain":"{a} − {b} = {=a-b}.","params":{"a":[8,12],"b":[2,7]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"(x^{a})^{b} = x^? What is the power?","answer":"a*b","hint":"Multiply the powers.","explain":"{a} × {b} = {=a*b}.","params":{"a":[2,5],"b":[2,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Why is x³ × x⁵ not x¹⁵?","choices":["There are only 3 + 5 = 8 factors of x","Because 15 is too big","You should subtract"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count the x's.","explain":"3 x's and 5 x's make 8 x's: x⁸."}],"explain":{"kid":["x³ means x × x × x. The small number counts how many x's are multiplied.","x³ × x⁵ puts 3 x's next to 5 more. That is 8 x's, so it is x⁸. You add the powers."],"adult":["Multiply: aᵐ × aⁿ = aᵐ⁺ⁿ. Divide: aᵐ ÷ aⁿ = aᵐ⁻ⁿ, because n factors cancel. Power of a power: (aᵐ)ⁿ = aᵐⁿ.","The laws need the same base. 2³ × 3² cannot be combined this way. A common slip is x³ × x⁵ = x¹⁵. Counting the factors shows it is x⁸."]},"familyTip":"Write x³ × x⁵ the long way, with every x. Count them together.","keyIdea":"When you multiply powers of the same base you add the powers, when you divide you subtract them, and a power of a power multiplies them.","visual":{"type":"expo","caption":"Choose a law and change the powers. Count the tiles to check the rule.","opts":{"mode":"laws","m":3,"n":2,"op":"mul"}}},"mt_mq4eGlJDk9":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"{b}^-1 = 1/? (give the denominator)","answer":"b","hint":"Flip it.","explain":"{b}⁻¹ = 1/{b}.","params":{"b":[2,12]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"4^-{n} = 1/? (give the denominator)","answer":"4**n","hint":"4ⁿ underneath.","explain":"4^{n} = {=4**n}.","params":{"n":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"10⁻² = ?","choices":["0.01","−100","0.2"],"answer":0,"hint":"1/10².","explain":"1/100 = 0.01."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Why is a⁰ = 1?","choices":["a³ ÷ a³ is a⁰ and also 1","Zero times anything is 0","It is just a rule with no reason"],"answer":0,"hint":"Use the divide law.","explain":"a³ ÷ a³ = a³⁻³ = a⁰, and any number ÷ itself = 1."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"x^{a} × x^-{b} = x^? What is the power?","answer":"a-b","hint":"Add the powers.","explain":"{a} + (−{b}) = {=a-b}.","params":{"a":[5,9],"b":[1,4]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"What is 2^-{n} × 2^{n}?","answer":"1","hint":"Powers add to 0.","explain":"−{n} + {n} = 0, and 2⁰ = 1.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Which is the largest?","choices":["2⁰","2⁻¹","2⁻³"],"answer":0,"hint":"1, 1/2, 1/8.","explain":"2⁰ = 1 is the largest."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"5⁰ = ?","choices":["1","0","5"],"answer":0,"hint":"Follow the pattern.","explain":"Any non-zero number to the power 0 is 1."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"2^-{n} = 1/? (give the denominator)","answer":"2**n","hint":"2⁻ⁿ = 1/2ⁿ.","explain":"2^{n} = {=2**n}, so the answer is 1/{=2**n}.","params":{"n":[1,6]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"10^-{n} as a decimal: how many places after the point is the 1?","answer":"n","hint":"10⁻¹ = 0.1.","explain":"10⁻{n} has the 1 in place {n}.","params":{"n":[1,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Is 3⁻² negative?","choices":["No, it is 1/9","Yes, it is −9","Yes, it is −6"],"answer":0,"hint":"Negative power, not negative number.","explain":"3⁻² = 1/9, a small positive number."}],"explain":{"kid":["Look at 2³ = 8, 2² = 4, 2¹ = 2. Each time the power goes down by 1, we halve.","Halve once more: 2⁰ = 1. Then 2⁻¹ = 1/2 and 2⁻² = 1/4. A minus power means a fraction."],"adult":["Going down the pattern divides by the base each time, so a⁰ = 1 for any a that is not 0. The laws agree: a³ ÷ a³ = a⁰, and it also equals 1.","Continue the pattern: a⁻ⁿ = 1/aⁿ. So 10⁻² = 1/100 = 0.01 and 2⁻³ = 1/8. A negative power is small but still positive."]},"familyTip":"Write the powers of 10 down a page, from 1000 to 0.001. Label each with its power.","keyIdea":"Each step down in the power divides by the base, so a⁰ = 1 and a⁻ⁿ = 1/aⁿ.","visual":{"type":"expo","caption":"Step down the ladder. Each step divides by 2 and lowers the power by 1.","opts":{"mode":"ladder","base":2,"n":3}}},"mt_OcSj7J9q1t":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"Find the square root of {=r*r}, written as a power: {=r*r}^(1/2).","answer":"r","hint":"Square root.","explain":"√{=r*r} = {r}.","params":{"r":[3,15]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"What is {=r*r*r}^(2/3)?","answer":"r*r","hint":"Cube root, then square.","explain":"∛{=r*r*r} = {r} and {r}² = {=r*r}.","params":{"r":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Write ∛x as a power.","choices":["x^(1/3)","x³","x/3"],"answer":0,"hint":"Cube root is 1/3.","explain":"∛x = x^(1/3)."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"16^(3/4) = ?","choices":["8","12","64"],"answer":0,"hint":"Fourth root first.","explain":"16^(1/4) = 2 and 2³ = 8."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"x^(1/2) × x^(1/2) = x^? Give the power.","answer":1,"hint":"Add the powers.","explain":"1/2 + 1/2 = 1, so x¹ = x."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Why is 25^(1/2) = 5?","choices":["(25^(1/2))² = 25¹ = 25, and 5² = 25","25 ÷ 2 is about 5","Half of 10 is 5"],"answer":0,"hint":"Power of a power.","explain":"Squaring 25^(1/2) gives 25, so it must be 5."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Write x^(3/2) with a root.","choices":["(√x)³","∛(x²)","3√x/2"],"answer":0,"hint":"Denominator is the root.","explain":"The 2 means a square root, the 3 means cubed: (√x)³."}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"What is {=r*r}^(1/2)?","answer":"r","hint":"Which number squared gives it?","explain":"{r} × {r} = {=r*r}, so the answer is {r}.","params":{"r":[2,12]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"What is {=r*r*r}^(1/3)?","answer":"r","hint":"Which number cubed gives it?","explain":"{r}³ = {=r*r*r}, so the answer is {r}.","params":{"r":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"x^(1/2) is the same as…","choices":["√x","x/2","x²"],"answer":0,"hint":"A half power.","explain":"A power of 1/2 is a square root."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"8^(2/3) = ?","choices":["4","16","5.33"],"answer":0,"hint":"Cube root first, then square.","explain":"∛8 = 2 and 2² = 4."}],"explain":{"kid":["9 = 3 × 3. So 3 is the square root of 9. We can also write it as 9 to the power 1/2.","27 = 3 × 3 × 3. So the cube root of 27 is 3, and 27 to the power 1/3 is 3."],"adult":["Use the power-of-a-power law: (a^(1/n))ⁿ = a¹ = a. So a^(1/n) is the number that gives a when it is multiplied n times: the nth root.","For a^(m/n), take the root first, then the power: 8^(2/3) = (∛8)² = 2² = 4. Taking the root first keeps the numbers small."]},"familyTip":"Find numbers that are perfect squares and cubes, like 64. Then try 64 to the powers 1/2, 1/3 and 2/3.","keyIdea":"a^(1/n) is the nth root of a, and a^(m/n) is that root raised to the power m.","visual":{"type":"expo","caption":"Split 8 into equal factors. Change the root and the power to see a^(m/k).","opts":{"mode":"root","a":8,"k":3,"m":1}}},"mt_iHEB0g_nCc":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"x² = {=r*r}. What is the negative solution?","answer":"-r","hint":"Find the square root, then put a minus sign in front of it.","explain":"The square root of {=r*r} is {r}, so the negative solution is x = −{r}.","params":{"r":[2,12]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"x³ = -{=r*r*r}. What is x?","answer":"-r","hint":"Cubes keep the sign.","explain":"∛(−{=r*r*r}) = −{r}.","params":{"r":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"√2 is…","choices":["irrational","exactly 1.4","a whole number"],"answer":0,"hint":"Its decimal never stops.","explain":"√2 = 1.41421… never ends or repeats: irrational."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"x² = {=r*r}. What is the sum of the two solutions?","answer":"0","hint":"The two solutions are the same number, one positive and one negative.","explain":"The solutions are {r} and −{r}. Opposites always add to zero: {r} + (−{r}) = 0.","params":{"r":[2,12]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Solve x³ = 1000.","choices":["x = 10","x = ±10","x = 100"],"answer":0,"hint":"One cube root.","explain":"10³ = 1000, and only one real answer."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"On a graph of y = x², the line y = 9 crosses at…","choices":["x = −3 and x = 3","x = 3 only","x = 81"],"answer":0,"hint":"The y = x² curve is a mirror image on each side of the y-axis. Which numbers squared make 9?","explain":"3 × 3 = 9 and −3 × −3 = 9, so the line crosses the curve at x = −3 and x = 3."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"A square has area {=r*r} cm². How long is its side, in cm?","answer":"r","hint":"Length can't be negative.","explain":"√{=r*r} = {r} cm (ignore −{r} for a length).","params":{"r":[3,15]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"x² = {=r*r}. What is the positive solution?","answer":"r","hint":"Ask: which positive number times itself makes {=r*r}? That is the square root.","explain":"The square root of {=r*r} is {r}, so x = {r} or x = −{r}. The positive one is {r}.","params":{"r":[2,12]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"x³ = {=r*r*r}. What is x?","answer":"r","hint":"Ask: which number times itself three times makes {=r*r*r}? That is the cube root.","explain":"The cube root of {=r*r*r} is {r}, because {r} × {r} × {r} = {=r*r*r}. A cube has only one real root.","params":{"r":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"How many real solutions does x² = 49 have?","choices":["2","1","0"],"answer":0,"hint":"7 × 7 = 49. Can a negative number squared also make 49?","explain":"Both 7 × 7 and −7 × −7 make 49, so there are two solutions: 7 and −7."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"x² = −4 has…","choices":["no real solutions","x = −2","x = ±2"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can a square be negative?","explain":"A square is never negative, so there is no real solution."}],"explain":{"kid":["6 × 6 = 36. But −6 × −6 = 36 too! So x² = 36 has two answers: 6 and −6.","For cubes, 3 × 3 × 3 = 27, but −3 cubed is −27. So x³ = 27 has just one answer, 3.","To undo a square, use a square root and remember the ± sign. To undo a cube, use a cube root."],"adult":["To solve x² = p, take the square root of both sides and keep both signs: x = ±√p. If p is negative there is no real answer, because a square is never negative.","To solve x³ = p, take the cube root: x = ∛p. Cubes keep the sign, so ∛(−8) = −2. Some roots, like √2, are irrational. Their decimals never end or repeat."]},"familyTip":"Draw y = x² and a flat line y = 9 on squared paper. Where do they meet?","keyIdea":"x² = p has two solutions, ±√p, when p is positive; x³ = p has exactly one, ∛p.","visual":{"type":"roots","caption":"Drag the level line up and down. Watch where it meets y = x²: two points, one, or none.","opts":{"k":2,"p":9,"xr":[-5,5],"yr":[-4,25]}}},"mt_Vn2yQGKnvi":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"(x − {a})(x + {b}) = x² + kx + c. What is c?","answer":"-a*b","hint":"Watch the sign.","explain":"−{a} × {b} = {=-a*b}.","params":{"a":[1,9],"b":[1,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"(x − {a})(x + {b}) = x² + kx + c. What is k?","answer":"b-a","hint":"−{a}x + {b}x.","explain":"{b} − {a} = {=b-a}.","params":{"a":[1,9],"b":[1,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"({p}x + 1)(x + {q}) = ax² + bx + c. What is a?","answer":"p","hint":"x² terms only.","explain":"{p}x × x = {p}x².","params":{"p":[2,6],"q":[1,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"(x + 1)³ = ?","choices":["x³ + 3x² + 3x + 1","x³ + 1","x³ + 3x + 1"],"answer":0,"hint":"Multiply two brackets first.","explain":"(x + 1)² = x² + 2x + 1; times (x + 1) gives x³ + 3x² + 3x + 1."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"What degree is (x² + 1)(x³ − 2)?","choices":["5","6","3"],"answer":0,"hint":"Highest powers multiply.","explain":"x² × x³ = x⁵, degree 5."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"(x + 3)² = ?","choices":["x² + 6x + 9","x² + 9","x² + 3x + 9"],"answer":0,"hint":"(x + 3)(x + 3).","explain":"x² + 3x + 3x + 9 = x² + 6x + 9."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"(x + {a})(x − {a}) = x² + kx + c. What is k?","answer":"0","hint":"The middle terms cancel.","explain":"{a}x − {a}x = 0.","params":{"a":[1,9]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"(x + {a})(x + {b}) = x² + kx + c. What is k?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Add the two middle boxes.","explain":"{a}x + {b}x = {=a+b}x.","params":{"a":[1,9],"b":[1,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"(x + {a})(x + {b}) = x² + kx + c. What is c?","answer":"a*b","hint":"Multiply the numbers.","explain":"{a} × {b} = {=a*b}.","params":{"a":[1,9],"b":[1,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"How many products before simplifying (x + 1)(x² + 2x + 3)?","choices":["6","5","3"],"answer":0,"hint":"2 terms × 3 terms.","explain":"2 × 3 = 6."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"(x − 1)(x² + x + 1) = ?","choices":["x³ − 1","x³ + 1","x³ − x − 1"],"answer":0,"hint":"Most terms cancel.","explain":"x³ + x² + x − x² − x − 1 = x³ − 1."}],"explain":{"kid":["(x + 2)(x + 3) means x + 2 lots of x + 3. Draw a grid: put x and 2 down the side, x and 3 along the top.","Fill each box by multiplying: x², 3x, 2x, 6. Add the like terms: x² + 5x + 6."],"adult":["Every term of the first bracket multiplies every term of the second. A binomial times a trinomial makes 2 × 3 = 6 products before you simplify.","(x − 1)(x² + x + 1) = x³ + x² + x − x² − x − 1 = x³ − 1. A grid keeps track of each product and its sign. For three brackets, multiply two first, then the third."]},"familyTip":"Multiply 12 × 13 with a grid: (10 + 2)(10 + 3). It's the same method as (x + 2)(x + 3).","keyIdea":"To multiply polynomials, multiply every term in one by every term in the other, then collect like terms.","visual":{"type":"grid","caption":"Each box is one term times one term. Change the numbers and watch the like terms collect.","opts":{"a":[1,-1],"b":[1,1,1]}}},"mt_zt4q9lu5va":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"(5x² + x − 4) − (2x² − 3x + 1) = ?","choices":["3x² + 4x − 5","3x² − 2x − 3","3x² + 4x − 3"],"answer":0,"hint":"Flip every sign in the second.","explain":"5x² − 2x² = 3x², x + 3x = 4x, −4 − 1 = −5."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"({a}x − {b}) − ({c}x − {d}). What is the constant term?","answer":"d-b","hint":"−{b} − (−{d}).","explain":"−{b} + {d} = {=d-b}.","params":{"a":[3,9],"b":[1,9],"c":[1,3],"d":[1,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"({a}x² + {b}x) + ({c}x² − {b}x). What is the x coefficient?","answer":"0","hint":"They cancel.","explain":"{b} − {b} = 0.","params":{"a":[1,9],"b":[1,9],"c":[1,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which pair are like terms?","choices":["4xy and −xy","x²y and xy²","3x and 3"],"answer":0,"hint":"Same letters, same powers.","explain":"4xy and −xy match exactly."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"(x³ + 2) + (x² − 2) = ?","choices":["x³ + x²","2x⁵","x³ + x² + 4"],"answer":0,"hint":"Nothing to combine except numbers.","explain":"2 − 2 = 0, so x³ + x²."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"({a}x² + 1) − ({b}x² + 1). What is the x² coefficient?","answer":"a-b","hint":"Subtract.","explain":"{a} − {b} = {=a-b}.","params":{"a":[6,12],"b":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"A common mistake in (6x + 2) − (x − 4) is getting…","choices":["5x − 2","5x + 6","7x − 2"],"answer":0,"hint":"Forgetting to flip −4.","explain":"The right answer is 5x + 6; forgetting the sign gives 5x − 2."}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"({a}x² + 2x) + ({b}x² + x). What is the x² coefficient?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Add like terms.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b}.","params":{"a":[2,9],"b":[2,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"({a}x + 5) − ({b}x + 1). What is the x coefficient?","answer":"a-b","hint":"Subtract like terms.","explain":"{a} − {b} = {=a-b}.","params":{"a":[5,12],"b":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Are 3x² and 3x like terms?","choices":["No, the powers differ","Yes, both have 3","Yes, both have x"],"answer":0,"hint":"Same letter AND same power.","explain":"x² and x have different powers."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"−(2x − 3) = ?","choices":["−2x + 3","−2x − 3","2x + 3"],"answer":0,"hint":"Change every sign.","explain":"−2x and +3."}],"explain":{"kid":["Like terms have the same letter and the same power, like 3x² and 5x². You can add them: 8x².","x² and x are not like terms. Keep them apart, like apples and oranges."],"adult":["To add, group like terms: (3x² + 2x − 1) + (x² − 5x + 4) = 4x² − 3x + 3.","To subtract, the minus sign hits every term in the second bracket. So (5x² + x − 4) − (2x² − 3x + 1) = 5x² + x − 4 − 2x² + 3x − 1 = 3x² + 4x − 5."]},"familyTip":"Write terms on sticky notes. Group the ones that are like terms, then add them up.","keyIdea":"Add or subtract polynomials by combining like terms; when subtracting, change the sign of every term in the second polynomial.","visual":{"type":"tiles","caption":"(5x² + x − 4) − (2x² − 3x + 1) with tiles. Subtracting flips the second bracket; Combine joins like tiles and cancels zero pairs.","opts":{"a":[5,1,-4],"b":[2,-3,1],"op":"-"}}}});
